# 2D Enemy Patrol and Chase AI

A simple enemy can move between waypoints and chase a target inside a range. This sample uses Rigidbody2D and Transform positions for target detection; it does not implement line-of-sight occlusion.

## Setup
Create an enemy with Rigidbody2D and Collider2D. Assign two waypoint Transforms and a target. Freeze Z rotation if the enemy should stay upright. Choose whether the enemy moves by physics or by Transform; this example uses Rigidbody2D.MovePosition.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class EnemyPatrolChase2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointA;
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointB;
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float patrolSpeed = 2f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float chaseSpeed = 3f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float detectionRange = 5f;

        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private bool towardB = true;

        private void Awake() => body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            Vector2 current = body.position;
            bool chasing = target != null &&
                ((Vector2)target.position - current).sqrMagnitude <= detectionRange * detectionRange;

            Vector2 destination;
            float speed;
            if (chasing)
            {
                destination = target.position;
                speed = chaseSpeed;
            }
            else
            {
                if (pointA == null || pointB == null) return;
                destination = towardB ? (Vector2)pointB.position : (Vector2)pointA.position;
                speed = patrolSpeed;
            }

            Vector2 next = Vector2.MoveTowards(current, destination, speed * Time.fixedDeltaTime);
            body.MovePosition(next);

            if (!chasing && Vector2.Distance(next, destination) <= 0.05f)
                towardB = !towardB;
        }
    }

## Extensions
Add a line-of-sight raycast for walls, a return-to-patrol delay, attack range and cooldown, and explicit states when behavior becomes complex. Do not allow a separate movement script to also move the same Rigidbody2D.

## Common mistakes
- Waypoints are missing.
- Rigidbody2D settings conflict with direct Transform changes elsewhere.
- Chase range is checked using distance from the wrong origin.
- Enemy chases through walls because only distance is checked.

# 2D Sprite Animation and Animator

## Import and create clips
1. Set sprite sheet Texture Type to Sprite (2D and UI), Sprite Mode to Multiple, then Apply.
2. Open Sprite Editor, slice the sheet, then Apply.
3. Select sliced sprites in order and create an animation clip.
4. Keep Pixels Per Unit and sprite pivots consistent to prevent size changes and wobble.
5. Inspect Animation window keyframes and remove accidental keys on properties the clip should not control.

## Animator setup
Add Animator and assign an Animator Controller. Create parameters such as Speed (Float), Grounded (Bool), and Jump (Trigger). Use explicit transition conditions. Disable Has Exit Time for immediate locomotion changes; enable it when a clip should finish first. A Blend Tree can smoothly blend idle/walk/run. Names and types must match exactly.

## Parameter driver
Save as SpriteAnimatorDriver.cs and attach to the Animator object.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class SpriteAnimatorDriver : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Animator animator;
        private static readonly int SpeedHash = Animator.StringToHash("Speed");
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (animator == null) animator = GetComponent<Animator>();
        }
        public void SetMoveSpeed(float speed)
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetFloat(SpeedHash, Mathf.Abs(speed));
        }
        public void SetGrounded(bool grounded)
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetBool("Grounded", grounded);
        }
        public void TriggerJump()
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetTrigger("Jump");
        }
    }

This drives parameters; it does not create states or transitions. Configure those in the Animator window.

## Sorting and troubleshooting
SpriteRenderer uses Sorting Layer and Order in Layer. For top-down games, sorting by Y may be useful. Check for missing controllers, misspelled parameters, wrong parameter types, disabled Loop Time, inconsistent pivots, and Animator/gameplay code both writing the same Transform property.

# 2D Sprite Flipping and Aiming

## Flip a character horizontally
Use SpriteRenderer.flipX when only the rendered sprite needs to face the other direction. This avoids changing Transform scale and introducing negative-scale side effects.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class SpriteFacing2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private SpriteRenderer spriteRenderer;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float deadZone = 0.05f;

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (spriteRenderer == null) spriteRenderer = GetComponentInChildren<SpriteRenderer>();
        }

        public void SetHorizontalMove(float x)
        {
            if (spriteRenderer == null || Mathf.Abs(x) < deadZone) return;
            spriteRenderer.flipX = x < 0f;
        }
    }

Call SetHorizontalMove with the character's horizontal movement input or velocity. If the character has a weapon/child transform that must also face the aim direction, flip or rotate that transform separately.

## Aim toward mouse
For a 2D world on the XY plane and a standard camera facing that plane, convert the mouse to world coordinates. Use camera-space depth and check the returned coordinate plane. A simple screen-to-world conversion must use a depth that intersects the gameplay plane.

    Vector3 mouse = Camera.main.ScreenToWorldPoint(
        new Vector3(Mouse.current.position.ReadValue().x,
                    Mouse.current.position.ReadValue().y,
                    Mathf.Abs(Camera.main.transform.position.z)));
    Vector2 aim = (Vector2)mouse - (Vector2)weapon.position;
    if (aim.sqrMagnitude > 0.0001f)
        weapon.right = aim.normalized;

This snippet assumes a conventional 2D camera at negative Z and gameplay around Z = 0. It requires the Input System namespace and a Mouse device. For gamepad aiming, use a direction vector from the stick instead.

## Common mistakes
- Sprite is mirrored but colliders/child weapon are not positioned as intended.
- Transform scale is negated and creates hierarchy side effects.
- Mouse depth does not intersect the gameplay plane.
- Code rotates the whole Rigidbody root and changes physics behavior.

# 2D Audio: Music and Sound Effects

AudioListener is usually on the camera; keep one active listener. AudioSource plays clips and configures volume, pitch, looping, spatial blend, and mixer routing. AudioMixer groups sources and controls effects/volume centrally. For conventional 2D effects, set Spatial Blend to 0.

## One-shot service
Create an AudioService object, add an AudioSource, disable Play On Awake, and assign a short confirm clip. Save this as AudioService.cs.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class AudioService : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public static AudioService Instance { get; private set; }
        [SerializeField] private AudioSource source;
        [SerializeField] private AudioClip confirmClip;
        [SerializeField, Range(0f, 1f)] private float volume = 1f;
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (Instance != null && Instance != this)
            {
                Destroy(gameObject);
                return;
            }
            Instance = this;
            if (source == null) source = GetComponent<AudioSource>();
        }
        public void PlayConfirm()
        {
            if (source != null && confirmClip != null)
                source.PlayOneShot(confirmClip, volume);
        }
    }

Call AudioService.Instance?.PlayConfirm() from UI/gameplay code. This sample does not persist across scenes. If adding DontDestroyOnLoad, prevent duplicates and deliberately manage lifetime.

## Import and performance
Short effects may use Decompress On Load or Compressed In Memory depending on latency, memory, and CPU. Long music often suits streaming or compressed-in-memory settings. Profile on target hardware. Pool or cap sources for rapid repeated sounds.

Common mistakes: no listener, multiple listeners, no clip/play call, Spatial Blend incorrectly set, mixer muted, or playback occurring while audio is paused.

# 2D Camera Bounds and Level Confinement

A follow camera should stay inside level bounds. A simple clamp computes the camera's orthographic half-height and half-width, then limits its center position so the view stays inside the rectangle.

## Clamp an orthographic camera
Attach to the Camera or camera rig. Assign a target and world-space minimum/maximum bounds. Assumes a 2D orthographic camera and rectangular level bounds.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Camera))]
    public sealed class CameraBounds2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 minBounds;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 maxBounds;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 offset;
        private Camera cam;

        private void Awake() => cam = GetComponent<Camera>();

        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (target == null || !cam.orthographic) return;

            float halfHeight = cam.orthographicSize;
            float halfWidth = halfHeight * cam.aspect;

            float minX = minBounds.x + halfWidth;
            float maxX = maxBounds.x - halfWidth;
            float minY = minBounds.y + halfHeight;
            float maxY = maxBounds.y - halfHeight;

            // When the view is wider/taller than the level, center it on that axis.
            float x = minX > maxX ? (minBounds.x + maxBounds.x) * 0.5f
                                  : Mathf.Clamp(target.position.x + offset.x, minX, maxX);
            float y = minY > maxY ? (minBounds.y + maxBounds.y) * 0.5f
                                  : Mathf.Clamp(target.position.y + offset.y, minY, maxY);

            transform.position = new Vector3(x, y, transform.position.z);
        }
    }

Set min/max to the actual world-space rectangle around the playable level, not the player's bounds. If using a separate smooth follow script, combine follow and clamping in one controller or ensure update order is intentional. Cinemachine Confiner may be preferable for irregular level boundaries.

## Common mistakes
- Using pixel bounds instead of world coordinates.
- Forgetting that camera half-width depends on aspect ratio.
- Clamping before smoothing, then smoothing moves the camera outside the bounds.
- The level is smaller than the camera view, requiring centering rather than an impossible clamp.

# 2D Camera: Follow, Framing, and Pixel Art

For an orthographic camera, Size is half the vertical world-space view height. Size 5 means 10 world units high; visible width depends on aspect ratio.

## Smooth follow
Save as CameraFollow2D.cs and attach to Main Camera. Assign the player Transform. A common convention is gameplay at Z = 0 and camera at Z = -10.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class CameraFollow2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float smoothTime = 0.12f;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 offset = new Vector2(0f, 1f);
        [SerializeField] private bool followX = true;
        [SerializeField] private bool followY = true;
        private Vector3 velocity;
        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (target == null) return;
            Vector3 current = transform.position;
            Vector3 desired = new Vector3(
                followX ? target.position.x + offset.x : current.x,
                followY ? target.position.y + offset.y : current.y,
                current.z);
            transform.position = Vector3.SmoothDamp(current, desired, ref velocity, smoothTime);
        }
    }

LateUpdate is a common place for camera following. For physics-driven targets, test interpolation and consider Cinemachine.

## Pixel art checklist
- Use consistent Pixels Per Unit.
- Consider the 2D Pixel Perfect package/component.
- Match reference resolution and art PPU.
- Avoid fractional camera motion if crisp pixel alignment is required.
- Test target resolutions and aspect ratios.

A follow script does not constrain the camera to level bounds. Use Cinemachine confiners or calculate bounds using camera half-width and half-height. Use a separate rig/offset for shake so shake and follow do not fight.

Common mistakes: missing target, wrong Z convention, confusing Size with full view height, excessive smoothing, and sub-pixel blur.

# Inspector Attributes and Serialization Reference

Unity serializes supported fields on MonoBehaviours and ScriptableObjects. Ordinary C# properties are not automatically shown or serialized by the default Inspector.

## Common attributes

    [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    [Header("Movement")]
    [Tooltip("World units per second.")]
    [Min(0f)] [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;
    [Range(0f, 1f)] [SerializeField] private float volume = 1f;
    [TextArea] [SerializeField] private string notes;
    [Space] [SerializeField] private Transform target;
    [HideInInspector] public int runtimeCounter;
    [System.NonSerialized] public float transientValue;

- SerializeField: serialize a supported private field and show it in Inspector.
- Header: section heading.
- Tooltip: hover description.
- Min: indicates a minimum for supported Inspector workflows; validate in code when correctness is important.
- Range: numeric slider.
- TextArea: multiline text field.
- Space: visual spacing.
- HideInInspector: hide a serialized field from Inspector.
- NonSerialized: exclude a field from Unity serialization.

## Component requirements and editor commands

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class NeedsBody : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [ContextMenu("Reset Runtime State")]
        private void ResetRuntimeState()
        {
            // Reset values for a convenient editor command.
        }
    }

RequireComponent helps ensure dependencies are present when the component is added, but runtime validation is still useful. ContextMenu exposes a method in the component Inspector menu. Avoid destructive actions that are easy to trigger accidentally.

## Renaming serialized fields
Use UnityEngine.Serialization.FormerlySerializedAs when renaming a field and preserving existing values matters.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.Serialization;

    public sealed class ExampleSettings : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [FormerlySerializedAs("oldSpeed")]
        [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    }

## Serialization rules
- Unity's built-in serializer supports a specific set of fields and collection patterns.
- Dictionaries, arbitrary properties, and many nested generic types are not supported directly by the default serializer.
- Avoid static fields for Inspector configuration.
- Serialized object references may become missing if assets/objects are deleted or moved.
- After changing serialized field names/types, inspect existing prefabs/scenes for lost data.

# C# Essentials for Unity Scripts

This is a Unity-focused reminder, not a complete C# language specification.

## Common types
- int: whole numbers and counters.
- float: decimal values used by most Unity math.
- bool: true/false state.
- string: text.
- Vector2 / Vector3: coordinates or directions.
- Quaternion: rotation representation.
- GameObject: scene object; Component: behavior attached to an object.
- Transform: position, rotation, and scale.
- List<T>: growable ordered collection; Dictionary<TKey,TValue>: keyed lookup collection.

## Fields, properties, and methods

    [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    public int Score { get; private set; }

    public void AddScore(int amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0) return;
        Score += amount;
    }

SerializeField makes a private field editable in the Inspector. A property controls code access, but Unity's default Inspector serialization does not serialize ordinary properties. Use fields for serialized data and properties for runtime access when appropriate.

## Branching and loops

    if (health <= 0f)
    {
        // Handle death.
    }
    else
    {
        // Continue playing.
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < items.Count; i++)
    {
        Debug.Log(items[i]);
    }

Use return to exit a method early, break to exit a loop, and continue to skip to the next loop iteration. Check collection length before indexing.

## Null and reference safety
A Unity reference may be missing, destroyed, or not assigned. Check references before use:

    if (target == null)
    {
        Debug.LogWarning("Target is missing.", this);
        return;
    }

NullReferenceException means code accessed a member through a missing reference. Trace where it should have been assigned or created.

## Common misconceptions
- = assigns; == compares.
- && requires both conditions; || needs either condition.
- Integer division can discard the fractional part; use floating-point operands when needed.
- Arrays have fixed length; List<T> can grow.
- public does not guarantee that Unity serializes a field if its type/shape is unsupported.
- MonoBehaviour scripts normally attach to GameObjects; ScriptableObjects are asset-style data objects.
- Standard MonoBehaviour class names should match their C# filenames.

# Prefabs, ScriptableObjects, and Data

## Prefabs
A prefab is a reusable serialized GameObject hierarchy. Drag a configured scene object into the Project window to create one. Use prefabs for enemies, projectiles, pickups, effects, and repeatable UI.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class Spawner2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
        [SerializeField] private Transform spawnPoint;
        public GameObject Spawn()
        {
            if (prefab == null)
            {
                Debug.LogError("Assign a prefab in the Inspector.", this);
                return null;
            }
            Transform point = spawnPoint != null ? spawnPoint : transform;
            return Instantiate(prefab, point.position, point.rotation);
        }
    }

Instantiate creates a runtime instance; it does not create a Project asset.

## Prefab workflow
- Edit the prefab asset when every instance should inherit the change.
- Inspect Overrides for scene-specific differences.
- Use variants for related types that share a base.
- Avoid scene-only references in reusable prefab assets unless assigned at runtime.
- Keep required components and dependencies on the prefab where practical.

## ScriptableObjects
Use ScriptableObjects for shared authored definitions such as weapons, enemies, levels, and items.

    using UnityEngine;
    [CreateAssetMenu(menuName = "Game/Weapon Definition")]
    public sealed class WeaponDefinition : ScriptableObject
    {
        public string displayName = "Basic";
        [Min(0f)] public float damage = 10f;
        [Min(0f)] public float cooldown = 0.5f;
        public Sprite icon;
    }

Create the asset through the Project window's Create menu. ScriptableObject assets are shared authored data, not a player save system. Avoid mutating project asset values as if they were per-player progress.

## Choosing storage
- Serialized component fields: per-instance configuration.
- Prefab: reusable object/component setup.
- ScriptableObject: shared authored definitions.
- JSON or platform save API: runtime player progress.
- Static fields/singletons: convenient global access but can hide dependencies and complicate testing.

# Unity C# Lifecycle and Script Architecture

| Callback | Typical purpose | Important detail |
|---|---|---|
| Awake | Cache components and initialize local state | Do not assume other objects have run Start |
| OnEnable | Subscribe to events or enable actions | Runs on each enable |
| Start | Setup needing other objects' Awake initialization | Runs once before first frame update while enabled |
| Update | Frame-based input and non-physics logic | Frame rate varies |
| FixedUpdate | Physics-step work | Zero, one, or multiple times per rendered frame |
| LateUpdate | Camera follow after ordinary updates | Other-script ordering may still need design |
| OnDisable | Unsubscribe and disable actions | Can run many times |
| OnDestroy | Final cleanup | Other objects may already be gone |

Collision and trigger callbacks require correct colliders, Rigidbody configuration, and matching 2D/3D physics types.

## Good habits
- Cache components in Awake instead of searching every frame.
- Keep movement, health, UI, and audio responsibilities separate.
- Use private fields with SerializeField for Inspector configuration.
- Validate references and log clear errors.
- Subscribe in OnEnable and unsubscribe in OnDisable if re-enable cycles are possible.
- Capture short-lived input in Update or action callbacks, then pass it to physics logic.
- Do not rely on unspecified order between unrelated scripts.

## Component pattern

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class ExampleComponent : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Rigidbody2D body;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float moveSpeed = 5f;
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (body == null) body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            if (body == null) Debug.LogError("Rigidbody2D is required.", this);
        }
        private void Update()
        {
            // Read frame-based input here.
        }
        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            if (body == null) return;
            // Apply physics changes here.
        }
    }

Update runs per rendered frame; FixedUpdate uses the fixed timestep in Time settings. Never assume they run one-to-one. Coroutines run on Unity's main loop and are not threads. WaitForSeconds is scaled by timeScale; WaitForSecondsRealtime is not.

# Tags, GameObject Layers, Sorting Layers, and LayerMasks

These systems have related names but different purposes. Choose the one matching the problem.

## Tags
Tags identify a GameObject with one label, such as Player, Enemy, or Checkpoint. Use CompareTag for comparisons. Tags are not a collision filter; they answer identity questions.

    if (other.CompareTag("Player"))
    {
        // Handle a player entering a trigger.
    }

The tag must exist and be assigned exactly as the code expects.

## GameObject Layers
Layers group objects for cameras, physics filtering, and raycasts. A Camera Culling Mask decides which layers it renders. The Physics 2D or 3D collision matrix controls which layer pairs can interact.

## LayerMask
LayerMask stores a set of selected layers as bits. In the Inspector, choose layer names through the mask dropdown. In code, LayerMask.GetMask("Ground", "Enemy") is safer than manually typing bit values. Ensure names exist and match capitalization.

## Sorting Layers
Sorting Layers and Order in Layer control 2D renderer draw order. They do not determine physics collision. Changing a GameObject's Layer will not fix SpriteRenderer order, and changing its Sorting Layer will not stop a collider from colliding.

## Example: raycast filter

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class LayerFilteredRay2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask targetLayers;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float distance = 5f;

        public bool HasTarget()
        {
            RaycastHit2D hit = Physics2D.Raycast(
                transform.position, transform.right, distance, targetLayers);
            return hit.collider != null;
        }
    }

## Troubleshooting sequence
1. Verify the object's tag/layer in Inspector.
2. Check camera culling mask when objects do not render.
3. Check Layer Collision Matrix when physics callbacks do not fire.
4. Check the query's LayerMask when raycasts/overlaps miss.
5. Check Sorting Layer and Order in Layer when 2D sprites render behind the wrong object.

# Unity Time, Coroutines, and Invoke

## Time values
- Time.deltaTime: elapsed scaled time since the previous rendered frame. Use for frame-based movement.
- Time.fixedDeltaTime: fixed physics timestep. Use when computing per-physics-step motion.
- Time.time: scaled game time; stops advancing when timeScale is zero.
- Time.unscaledDeltaTime: frame time unaffected by timeScale; useful for pause UI.
- Time.timeScale: global scaled-time multiplier. Zero pauses scaled time, not every Unity system.

## Frame-rate-independent movement
For an object moved directly by Transform rather than a Rigidbody:

    transform.position += direction.normalized * speed * Time.deltaTime;

Without deltaTime, distance changes with frame rate. For Rigidbody movement, use its physics API in FixedUpdate instead of combining Transform movement and simulation.

## Coroutine example
Save as TimedEffect.cs.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class TimedEffect : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float duration = 1f;
        private Coroutine activeRoutine;

        public void Begin()
        {
            if (activeRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(activeRoutine);
            activeRoutine = StartCoroutine(Run());
        }

        private IEnumerator Run()
        {
            // Apply the effect here.
            yield return new WaitForSeconds(duration);
            // Remove the effect here.
            activeRoutine = null;
        }

        private void OnDisable()
        {
            if (activeRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(activeRoutine);
            activeRoutine = null;
        }
    }

WaitForSeconds uses scaled time. Use WaitForSecondsRealtime for real time even while paused. Coroutines are not threads; blocking work inside one still blocks the main thread.

## Invoke and cancellation
Invoke(nameof(MethodName), delay) schedules a delayed method; CancelInvoke cancels pending Invoke calls on that component. For sequences, cancellation, or data passed between steps, coroutines are often clearer.

# 2D Checkpoints and Level Progression

A checkpoint stores a respawn position after the player touches it. Keep checkpoint state separate from its visual indicator. Decide whether progress resets after scene reload or persists in a save file.

## Checkpoint trigger
Attach this script to an object with Collider2D set to Is Trigger. Assign a player tag and optionally a child respawnPoint Transform.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Checkpoint2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private string playerTag = "Player";
        [SerializeField] private Transform respawnPoint;
        [SerializeField] private GameObject activatedVisual;
        private bool activated;

        private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
        {
            if (activated || !other.CompareTag(playerTag)) return;

            PlayerRespawn2D respawn = other.GetComponentInParent<PlayerRespawn2D>();
            if (respawn == null) return;

            Transform point = respawnPoint != null ? respawnPoint : transform;
            respawn.SetCheckpoint(point.position);
            activated = true;
            if (activatedVisual != null) activatedVisual.SetActive(true);
        }
    }

## Player respawn component
Attach to the player root. Assign initialSpawnPoint if the initial spawn differs from the player's starting Transform.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class PlayerRespawn2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform initialSpawnPoint;
        [SerializeField] private Rigidbody2D body;
        private Vector2 checkpointPosition;

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (body == null) body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            checkpointPosition = initialSpawnPoint != null
                ? (Vector2)initialSpawnPoint.position
                : (Vector2)transform.position;
        }

        public void SetCheckpoint(Vector2 position) => checkpointPosition = position;

        public void Respawn()
        {
            if (body != null)
            {
                body.linearVelocity = Vector2.zero;
                body.angularVelocity = 0f;
                body.position = checkpointPosition;
            }
            else
            {
                transform.position = checkpointPosition;
            }
        }
    }

Call Respawn when health reaches zero. Decide separately whether to restore health, lives, and collected items. Using a nullable Transform instead of a zero-coordinate sentinel allows the world origin to be a valid spawn.

## Scene progression
Use SceneManager and include scenes in the active Build Profile. Store the next scene name/index deliberately; Project folder order does not determine build index.

## Common mistakes
- Trigger never fires because collider/layer/Rigidbody setup is wrong.
- Player tag does not match.
- Respawn leaves Rigidbody velocity intact.
- Health/death events are not connected to Respawn.
- Scene name or build index is not configured.

# 2D Damage, Knockback, and Temporary Invulnerability

This pattern uses a Rigidbody2D actor and a Health2D component. Call ReceiveHit only after a collision/weapon system confirms a hit. Keep damage validation separate from collision detection.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class DamageResponse2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Health2D health;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float invulnerabilitySeconds = 0.6f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float knockbackImpulse = 5f;
        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private bool invulnerable;
        private Coroutine invulnerabilityRoutine;

        private void Awake()
        {
            body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            if (health == null) health = GetComponent<Health2D>();
        }

        public void ReceiveHit(float damage, Vector2 sourcePosition)
        {
            if (invulnerable || health == null || health.CurrentHealth <= 0f) return;
            health.TakeDamage(damage);

            Vector2 away = (Vector2)transform.position - sourcePosition;
            if (away.sqrMagnitude < 0.0001f) away = Vector2.up;
            body.AddForce(away.normalized * knockbackImpulse, ForceMode2D.Impulse);

            if (invulnerabilityRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(invulnerabilityRoutine);
            invulnerabilityRoutine = StartCoroutine(InvulnerabilityWindow());
        }

        private IEnumerator InvulnerabilityWindow()
        {
            invulnerable = true;
            yield return new WaitForSeconds(invulnerabilitySeconds);
            invulnerable = false;
            invulnerabilityRoutine = null;
        }
    }

WaitForSeconds uses scaled time, so pausing the game pauses this timer. A visual blink effect can show invulnerability, but must not be the actual damage gate.

Common mistakes: health is on a parent but GetComponent checks only the same object, Rigidbody2D is missing, a timer restarts unexpectedly, or visual blinking is used instead of an invulnerability state.

# Health and damage system (2D)

This small reusable component tracks health, exposes a damage method, and raises an event when health changes. It does not assume a particular UI or respawn system.

## PlayerHealth.cs

```csharp
using System;
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class PlayerHealth : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField, Min(1)] private int maxHealth = 5;
    public int CurrentHealth { get; private set; }
    public int MaxHealth => maxHealth;
    public bool IsDead => CurrentHealth <= 0;

    public event Action<int, int> HealthChanged;
    public event Action Died;

    private void Awake()
    {
        CurrentHealth = maxHealth;
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }

    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        if (IsDead || amount <= 0) return;

        CurrentHealth = Mathf.Max(0, CurrentHealth - amount);
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);

        if (IsDead)
            Died?.Invoke();
    }

    public void Heal(int amount)
    {
        if (IsDead || amount <= 0) return;

        CurrentHealth = Mathf.Min(maxHealth, CurrentHealth + amount);
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }

    public void RestoreFullHealth()
    {
        CurrentHealth = maxHealth;
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }
}
```

## Connecting a UI

Subscribe to HealthChanged in the UI's OnEnable and unsubscribe in OnDisable. Update the bar only when the event fires, rather than searching for UI objects every frame. Subscribe to Died to open a game-over screen or request a respawn.

## Design notes

- Clamp health so it never falls below zero or above maximum.
- Ignore damage after death unless your game explicitly supports it.
- Decide whether healing can revive a dead character.
- For multiplayer, validate damage and health changes on the authoritative peer/server.

# Object pooling for 2D effects

Pooling can reduce repeated Instantiate/Destroy overhead for projectiles, particles, and short-lived effects. Measure before adding complexity.

## Basic pool

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class Pool2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
    [SerializeField, Min(1)] private int prewarm = 12;
    private readonly Queue<GameObject> pool = new();

    private void Awake()
    {
        if (prefab == null)
        {
            Debug.LogError("Assign a prefab.", this);
            enabled = false;
            return;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < prewarm; i++)
        {
            GameObject item = Instantiate(prefab, transform);
            item.SetActive(false);
            pool.Enqueue(item);
        }
    }

    public GameObject Get(Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)
    {
        GameObject item = pool.Count > 0 ? pool.Dequeue() : Instantiate(prefab);
        item.transform.SetPositionAndRotation(position, rotation);
        item.SetActive(true);
        return item;
    }

    public void Return(GameObject item)
    {
        if (item == null) return;
        item.SetActive(false);
        item.transform.SetParent(transform);
        pool.Enqueue(item);
    }
}
```

## Reset state on reuse

A pooled projectile must reset velocity, damage, lifetime, collision state, and any coroutine/timer state when activated. Ensure an object cannot be returned to the pool twice. Consider Unity's ObjectPool<T> API for more advanced projects.

# 2D Pickups and Respawning

## Pickup setup
Create a pickup GameObject with SpriteRenderer and Collider2D. Enable Is Trigger. Add a Rigidbody2D set to Kinematic if needed for the desired trigger setup. Put the player on a layer that the pickup detects.

## Collect once
Save as Pickup2D.cs.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Pickup2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0)] private int points = 1;
        [SerializeField] private string playerTag = "Player";
        [SerializeField] private GameObject visualRoot;
        private bool collected;

        private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
        {
            if (collected || !other.CompareTag(playerTag)) return;
            collected = true;

            ScoreCounter score = FindFirstObjectByType<ScoreCounter>();
            if (score != null) score.Add(points);

            if (visualRoot != null) visualRoot.SetActive(false);
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }

The example expects a ScoreCounter component; add the counter below. For a large game, prefer an explicit reference/event or player interface rather than searching the scene at collection time.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class ScoreCounter : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public int Score { get; private set; }
        public void Add(int amount)
        {
            if (amount > 0) Score += amount;
        }
    }

Set the player tag to exactly Player in the Tag Manager and assign it to the player. Alternatively, use layers or a component/interface check. If you want a pickup to respawn, do not Destroy it; disable its visual/collider and start a coroutine to reactivate it after a delay.

## Respawn pattern
Keep the pickup GameObject active so its coroutine host survives; disable only the child visuals and collider. After collecting, wait, restore visuals/collider, and reset the collected flag. If the whole pickup GameObject is deactivated, its coroutine will not continue.

## Common mistakes
- Tag spelling/capitalization differs.
- Trigger collider has no valid physics pair.
- Pickup can award points multiple times due to missing collected guard.
- A coroutine host is deactivated when its timer needs to complete.
- Destroying the object prevents respawning that same instance.

# 2D Save and Load with JSON

Use Application.persistentDataPath for small local progress. This example intentionally stores only simple fields. It is not secure storage; players can edit local save files.

## Data model and system
Save as LocalProgressSave.cs.

    using System;
    using System.IO;
    using UnityEngine;

    [Serializable]
    public sealed class LocalProgressData
    {
        public int coins;
        public int bestScore;
        public int unlockedLevel = 1;
    }

    public sealed class LocalProgressSave : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private string fileName = "progress.json";
        public LocalProgressData Data { get; private set; } = new LocalProgressData();
        private string PathToSave => Path.Combine(Application.persistentDataPath, fileName);

        public void Save()
        {
            try
            {
                string json = JsonUtility.ToJson(Data, true);
                File.WriteAllText(PathToSave, json);
            }
            catch (Exception e)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Could not save progress: {e.Message}", this);
            }
        }

        public void Load()
        {
            if (!File.Exists(PathToSave))
            {
                Data = new LocalProgressData();
                return;
            }

            try
            {
                Data = JsonUtility.FromJson<LocalProgressData>(File.ReadAllText(PathToSave))
                       ?? new LocalProgressData();
            }
            catch (Exception e)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Could not load progress: {e.Message}", this);
                Data = new LocalProgressData();
            }
        }

        private void OnApplicationPause(bool paused)
        {
            if (paused) Save();
        }
    }

Place one instance in a scene and call Load at startup and Save after meaningful progress changes. Mobile platforms may not call quit reliably, so don't depend only on OnApplicationQuit. Avoid using arbitrary player-controlled file names without validation.

## Serialization limits
JsonUtility handles serializable fields and supported types, but not arbitrary dictionaries or properties. Add a schema version and migration logic if the data format changes. Use stable item IDs rather than serializing direct references to scene objects.

## Testing
Test a first run with no file, normal save/load, invalid/corrupt JSON, and values at boundary cases. Local files are not appropriate for secrets or authoritative multiplayer currency.

# 2D Gameplay: Scene Changes, Pause, and Restart

Add every required scene to the active Build Profile. Scene names are case-sensitive; build indices depend on the configured scene list.

## Pause controller
Requires the Input System package. Attach to a game manager and assign a pause menu GameObject. Wire Resume and RestartCurrentScene to menu buttons.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;
    using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;
    public sealed class PauseController : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject pauseMenu;
        private bool paused;
        private void Start() => SetPaused(false);
        private void Update()
        {
            if (Keyboard.current != null && Keyboard.current.escapeKey.wasPressedThisFrame)
                SetPaused(!paused);
        }
        public void Resume() => SetPaused(false);
        public void RestartCurrentScene()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(SceneManager.GetActiveScene().buildIndex);
        }
        private void SetPaused(bool value)
        {
            paused = value;
            Time.timeScale = paused ? 0f : 1f;
            if (pauseMenu != null) pauseMenu.SetActive(paused);
        }
        private void OnDestroy() => Time.timeScale = 1f;
    }

Time.timeScale = 0 stops scaled physics and scaled waits, but Update still runs. WaitForSeconds uses scaled time; WaitForSecondsRealtime does not. Restore timeScale before scene changes. For gamepad/touch support, use a Pause action instead of hardcoding Escape. Avoid multiple systems independently controlling global timeScale.

# 2D Game State: Win, Lose, and Restart Flow

Keep the current game state in one place. For a small game, an enum can distinguish Playing, Paused, Won, and Lost; this is easier to reason about than several booleans that can conflict.

## Basic state manager
Save as GameState2D.cs and attach to a game manager. Connect UI buttons to RestartCurrentScene and ReturnToMenu. Add the menu scene to the active Build Profile.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;

    public sealed class GameState2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public enum State { Playing, Paused, Won, Lost }
        public State CurrentState { get; private set; } = State.Playing;

        [SerializeField] private GameObject winPanel;
        [SerializeField] private GameObject losePanel;
        [SerializeField] private string menuScene = "MainMenu";

        private void Start() => RefreshUI();

        public void Win()
        {
            if (CurrentState != State.Playing) return;
            CurrentState = State.Won;
            RefreshUI();
        }

        public void Lose()
        {
            if (CurrentState != State.Playing) return;
            CurrentState = State.Lost;
            RefreshUI();
        }

        public void RestartCurrentScene()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(SceneManager.GetActiveScene().buildIndex);
        }

        public void ReturnToMenu()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(menuScene);
        }

        private void RefreshUI()
        {
            if (winPanel != null) winPanel.SetActive(CurrentState == State.Won);
            if (losePanel != null) losePanel.SetActive(CurrentState == State.Lost);
        }
    }

This manager displays panels but does not automatically pause gameplay or stop movement. Connect a centralized pause/state-aware input gate if the character must stop after winning/losing. Avoid setting timeScale in several unrelated scripts.

## Testing checklist
Trigger Win and Lose twice, verify only the first valid result changes state, restart after pause, and test missing UI references. Decide whether pause and terminal states may coexist; if they can, model them deliberately rather than overwriting one enum value.

# Unity Input System: 2D Setup

Requirements: Unity 6 and the Input System package. Install it through Package Manager. If prompted, enable the new input backend and restart Unity.

## Create actions
1. Create Assets/Input/Gameplay.inputactions and a map named Gameplay.
2. Add Move as Value / Vector2. Bind a 2D Vector composite for WASD and arrows, plus gamepad left stick.
3. Add Jump as Button, bound to Space and a gamepad south button.
4. Add PlayerInput to the player, assign the asset, set Default Map to Gameplay, and choose Send Messages.

## Input reader
Save as PlayerInputReader2D.cs and attach beside PlayerInput.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;
    [RequireComponent(typeof(PlayerInput))]
    public sealed class PlayerInputReader2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public Vector2 Move { get; private set; }
        public bool JumpPressed { get; private set; }
        public void OnMove(InputValue value) => Move = value.Get<Vector2>();
        public void OnJump(InputValue value)
        {
            if (value.isPressed) JumpPressed = true;
        }
        public bool ConsumeJumpPressed()
        {
            bool result = JumpPressed;
            JumpPressed = false;
            return result;
        }
        private void OnDisable()
        {
            Move = Vector2.zero;
            JumpPressed = false;
        }
    }

With Send Messages, action names determine callback names: Move calls OnMove and Jump calls OnJump. A physics controller can read Move and consume JumpPressed in its physics logic.

## Common mistakes
- The action map is disabled or Default Map is wrong.
- Callback names do not match action names.
- The action has the wrong value type or missing bindings.
- A one-frame press is lost between Update and FixedUpdate.
- Legacy Input.GetAxis code is used with only the new backend enabled.

Official guide: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.inputsystem@latest

# 2D Touch and Mobile Input

This guide adds a basic on-screen joystick for mobile builds. It uses uGUI and Unity's EventSystem; create a Canvas, an EventSystem, a background Image, and a smaller handle Image. Put the handle under the background and assign both RectTransforms.

## Virtual joystick
Save as VirtualJoystick.cs and attach to the background Image. Its Image must receive raycasts.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.EventSystems;

    public sealed class VirtualJoystick : MonoBehaviour,
        IPointerDownHandler, IDragHandler, IPointerUpHandler
    {
        [SerializeField] private RectTransform background;
        [SerializeField] private RectTransform handle;
        [SerializeField, Min(1f)] private float handleRange = 60f;
        public Vector2 Value { get; private set; }

        public void OnPointerDown(PointerEventData eventData) => OnDrag(eventData);

        public void OnDrag(PointerEventData eventData)
        {
            if (background == null || handle == null) return;
            if (!RectTransformUtility.ScreenPointToLocalPointInRectangle(
                background, eventData.position, eventData.pressEventCamera, out Vector2 local))
                return;

            Vector2 radius = background.rect.size * 0.5f;
            if (radius.x <= 0f || radius.y <= 0f) return;
            Value = Vector2.ClampMagnitude(
                new Vector2(local.x / radius.x, local.y / radius.y), 1f);
            handle.anchoredPosition = new Vector2(Value.x * handleRange, Value.y * handleRange);
        }

        public void OnPointerUp(PointerEventData eventData)
        {
            Value = Vector2.zero;
            if (handle != null) handle.anchoredPosition = Vector2.zero;
        }
    }

Adjust handleRange to match the joystick's RectTransform units and inspect anchors/pivot. The component reports Value; a movement controller must read it and apply movement.

## Input backend and testing
- Use the Input System's UI Input Module if the project uses the new Input System.
- Test with actual screen aspect ratios and safe areas; fixed pixel positions can be clipped by notches/rounded corners.
- UI images need Raycast Target enabled to receive pointer events.
- Keep touch buttons large enough to tap and don't rely only on color for state.
- A virtual joystick is a UI control, not a full mobile control scheme; add action buttons for jump, interact, or fire.

Official Input System docs: https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.inputsystem@latest

# 2D Platformer: Jump Buffering and Coyote Time

Jump buffering remembers a jump press briefly before landing. Coyote time allows a jump shortly after walking off an edge. Both make platform controls more forgiving.

## Concept
- Jump buffer: start a short timer when Jump is pressed; allow the jump if the character becomes grounded before it expires.
- Coyote time: refresh a timer while grounded; allow jumping while it remains above zero after leaving ground.
- Clear timers deliberately after a successful jump so an old press doesn't trigger later.

## Integration pattern
This component shows the timer logic. Call SetJumpPressed from an input callback and SetGrounded from your grounded detector. Your movement controller should consume CanJump and apply its own velocity/force.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class JumpGrace2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float bufferDuration = 0.12f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float coyoteDuration = 0.1f;
        private float jumpBufferRemaining;
        private float coyoteRemaining;
        public bool CanJump => jumpBufferRemaining > 0f && coyoteRemaining > 0f;

        public void SetJumpPressed() => jumpBufferRemaining = bufferDuration;

        public void SetGrounded(bool grounded)
        {
            if (grounded) coyoteRemaining = coyoteDuration;
        }

        private void Update()
        {
            jumpBufferRemaining = Mathf.Max(0f, jumpBufferRemaining - Time.deltaTime);
            coyoteRemaining = Mathf.Max(0f, coyoteRemaining - Time.deltaTime);
        }

        public bool ConsumeJump()
        {
            if (!CanJump) return false;
            jumpBufferRemaining = 0f;
            coyoteRemaining = 0f;
            return true;
        }
    }

Use Time.deltaTime for gameplay timers that follow scaled time. If the game can pause, ensure the jump logic does not run unexpectedly while paused. Keep grounded detection in the controller/ground-check system so there is one authoritative grounded state.

## Common mistakes
- Grounded is not refreshed every physics/frame step.
- Jump input is never cleared after use.
- Timers use Update while the controller reads/clears them from a different script with unclear ordering.
- Coyote time is refreshed while airborne due to a faulty ground check.

# 2D Moving Platforms

Use Rigidbody2D for a platform that moves through the physics world. Add a BoxCollider2D and Rigidbody2D set to Kinematic. Assign two endpoint Transforms in the Inspector; this script caches their world positions at startup, so the endpoint objects may be children without following the platform forever.

## Back-and-forth platform
Save as MovingPlatform2D.cs and attach to the platform.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class MovingPlatform2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointA;
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointB;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float speed = 2f;

        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private Vector2 positionA;
        private Vector2 positionB;
        private bool endpointsValid;
        private bool movingToB = true;

        private void Awake()
        {
            body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            endpointsValid = pointA != null && pointB != null;
            if (endpointsValid)
            {
                positionA = pointA.position;
                positionB = pointB.position;
            }
            else
            {
                Debug.LogError("Assign both platform endpoints.", this);
            }
        }

        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            if (!endpointsValid) return;

            Vector2 target = movingToB ? positionB : positionA;
            Vector2 next = Vector2.MoveTowards(
                body.position, target, speed * Time.fixedDeltaTime);
            body.MovePosition(next);

            if (Vector2.Distance(next, target) < 0.01f)
                movingToB = !movingToB;
        }
    }

Set Rigidbody2D Body Type to Kinematic. Put endpoint Transforms where the platform's center should stop. MovePosition in FixedUpdate is preferable to writing Transform position each frame for a physics object.

## Carrying the player
First test Rigidbody2D interpolation and collider behavior. Common strategies include parenting while grounded or applying platform delta to player movement. Applying platform delta through the movement controller avoids many hierarchy issues, but needs integration with the player controller. Avoid parenting/unparenting every frame due to tiny grounding fluctuations.

## Common mistakes
- Endpoints are unassigned or accidentally placed at the same world position.
- The platform moves in Update while the player simulates in FixedUpdate.
- Player slides off due to friction or controller code.
- Collider shapes snag at edges.

# 2D Platformer Controller — Complete Setup

This controller uses the Input System package and a Dynamic Rigidbody2D. It includes horizontal movement, ground checking, jump buffering, and coyote time. It is a foundation; slopes, ladders, wall jumps, variable jump height, and moving platform carry are separate features.

## Scene setup
1. Create a Player with SpriteRenderer, Rigidbody2D, and a CapsuleCollider2D or BoxCollider2D.
2. Set Rigidbody2D Body Type to Dynamic and Freeze Rotation Z.
3. Choose Interpolate if rendered motion looks jittery; choose collision detection based on speed.
4. Create a Ground layer and assign it to solid ground colliders.
5. Add a child Transform called GroundCheck, positioned just below the player's feet.
6. Install Input System and create a Gameplay map with Move (Value / Vector2) and Jump (Button).
7. Assign the actions to the script. The groundLayers mask must include ground and exclude the player layer.

## Script: PlatformerController2D.cs

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class PlatformerController2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [Header("Movement")]
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float moveSpeed = 7f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float jumpVelocity = 13f;

        [Header("Ground check")]
        [SerializeField] private Transform groundCheck;
        [SerializeField, Min(0.01f)] private float groundRadius = 0.12f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask groundLayers;

        [Header("Jump forgiveness")]
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float jumpBufferTime = 0.12f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float coyoteTime = 0.1f;

        [Header("Input actions")]
        [SerializeField] private InputActionReference moveAction;
        [SerializeField] private InputActionReference jumpAction;

        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private float horizontal;
        private float jumpBufferRemaining;
        private float coyoteRemaining;
        private bool grounded;

        private void Awake()
        {
            body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
        }

        private void OnEnable()
        {
            if (moveAction != null) moveAction.action.Enable();

            if (jumpAction != null)
            {
                jumpAction.action.performed += OnJump;
                jumpAction.action.Enable();
            }
        }

        private void OnDisable()
        {
            if (jumpAction != null)
            {
                jumpAction.action.performed -= OnJump;
                jumpAction.action.Disable();
            }

            if (moveAction != null) moveAction.action.Disable();
        }

        private void Update()
        {
            horizontal = moveAction != null
                ? moveAction.action.ReadValue<Vector2>().x
                : 0f;
        }

        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            grounded = groundCheck != null &&
                Physics2D.OverlapCircle(groundCheck.position, groundRadius, groundLayers) != null;

            if (grounded)
                coyoteRemaining = coyoteTime;
            else
                coyoteRemaining = Mathf.Max(0f, coyoteRemaining - Time.fixedDeltaTime);

            Vector2 velocity = body.linearVelocity;
            velocity.x = horizontal * moveSpeed;

            if (jumpBufferRemaining > 0f && coyoteRemaining > 0f)
            {
                velocity.y = jumpVelocity;
                jumpBufferRemaining = 0f;
                coyoteRemaining = 0f;
            }
            else
            {
                jumpBufferRemaining = Mathf.Max(
                    0f, jumpBufferRemaining - Time.fixedDeltaTime);
            }

            body.linearVelocity = velocity;
        }

        private void OnJump(InputAction.CallbackContext context)
        {
            jumpBufferRemaining = jumpBufferTime;
        }

        private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
        {
            if (groundCheck == null) return;
            Gizmos.color = Color.yellow;
            Gizmos.DrawWireSphere(groundCheck.position, groundRadius);
        }
    }

## How it works
- Update reads movement input; FixedUpdate checks ground and writes Rigidbody2D velocity.
- Jump callback starts a short buffer timer so a press just before landing can trigger.
- Ground contact refreshes coyote time; leaving an edge doesn't instantly remove jump eligibility.
- When both timers overlap, the controller applies jump velocity and consumes both timers.

## Important details
InputActionReference actions are enabled/disabled by this component. Avoid having multiple components independently enable/disable the same shared action. Jump buffer/coyote timers use fixed-step countdowns. This sample replaces horizontal velocity directly, so it does not implement acceleration or friction-based deceleration.

## Troubleshooting
- Never grounded: move GroundCheck just below feet and verify groundLayers.
- Jump not detected: check action binding, action type, reference assignment, and active input map.
- Diagonal movement irrelevant for platformer horizontal axis; only Move.x is used.
- Player tips: freeze Rigidbody2D rotation Z.
- Fast player tunnels through thin platforms: inspect continuous collision detection and timestep.

# 2D Top-Down Movement

This example moves a character in four or eight directions with a Rigidbody2D. It uses the Input System package and keeps diagonal speed consistent.

## Setup
- Player: SpriteRenderer, Rigidbody2D (Dynamic), and an appropriate Collider2D.
- Freeze Rigidbody2D rotation Z if the sprite should remain upright.
- Create a Move action (Value / Vector2), bind WASD and arrow keys, and assign it to the script.
- Set Linear Drag/Damping according to the feel you want; damping can make the character coast.

## Script: TopDownMovement2D.cs

```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.InputSystem;

[RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
public sealed class TopDownMovement2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float speed = 5f;
    [SerializeField] private InputActionReference moveAction;

    private Rigidbody2D body;
    private Vector2 moveInput;

    private void Awake() => body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

    private void OnEnable()
    {
        if (moveAction != null) moveAction.action.Enable();
    }

    private void OnDisable()
    {
        if (moveAction != null) moveAction.action.Disable();
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        moveInput = moveAction != null
            ? Vector2.ClampMagnitude(moveAction.action.ReadValue<Vector2>(), 1f)
            : Vector2.zero;
    }

    private void FixedUpdate()
    {
        body.linearVelocity = moveInput * speed;
    }
}
```

## Why ClampMagnitude?
Without normalization/clamping, simultaneous horizontal and vertical input can have magnitude √2, making diagonal movement faster. ClampMagnitude keeps input magnitude at or below one.

## Alternative movement styles
- Set Rigidbody2D velocity for direct, responsive arcade movement.
- Use AddForce for acceleration-based movement; tune mass, force, and damping together.
- Use MovePosition for an intentionally kinematic controller, after checking its collision behaviour.
- Use Transform movement only when physics response is not required.

## Troubleshooting
If the player slides, reduce damping or stop velocity when input is zero. If the player passes through thin walls at high speed, inspect collision detection and fixed timestep settings. Do not apply both velocity control and force control without a clear reason.

# Performance and Object Pooling

Profile first. Common costs include frequent Instantiate/Destroy, per-frame allocations, broad physics queries, oversized textures, transparent overdraw, and frequent UI rebuilds.

## Simple GameObject pool
Attach to a pool manager, assign a prefab, and set initial size. Call Release when an effect/projectile finishes. Do not Destroy pooled instances.

    using System.Collections.Generic;
    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class SimpleObjectPool : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
        [SerializeField, Min(0)] private int initialSize = 10;
        private readonly Queue<GameObject> available = new();
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (prefab == null)
            {
                Debug.LogError("Pool prefab is missing.", this);
                enabled = false;
                return;
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < initialSize; i++) CreateOne();
        }
        private GameObject CreateOne()
        {
            GameObject item = Instantiate(prefab, transform);
            item.SetActive(false);
            available.Enqueue(item);
            return item;
        }
        public GameObject Get(Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)
        {
            if (available.Count == 0) CreateOne();
            GameObject item = available.Dequeue();
            item.transform.SetPositionAndRotation(position, rotation);
            item.SetActive(true);
            return item;
        }
        public void Release(GameObject item)
        {
            if (item == null) return;
            item.SetActive(false);
            item.transform.SetParent(transform);
            available.Enqueue(item);
        }
    }

This minimal pool does not prevent releasing an object twice; production pools should track ownership/state. Reset velocity, timers, targets, and gameplay state when reusing items.

## More practices
Cache component references, reuse collections, avoid allocations in tight loops, use layer masks, avoid scene-wide searches in gameplay loops, use sensible asset compression, simplify colliders, and profile the target build.

# Collision layers and triggers (2D)

## Layers vs tags

Use **layers** to control which objects can collide and to filter physics queries. Use **tags** to identify broad gameplay categories such as Player or Enemy. A layer is not a substitute for a gameplay identity.

## Configure collision

1. Create layers such as Player, Ground, Enemy, Hazard, and Collectible.
2. Assign each GameObject to the appropriate layer.
3. Open **Edit > Project Settings > Physics 2D**.
4. Review the Layer Collision Matrix and disable pairs that should never interact.
5. Test triggers and collision callbacks in Play Mode.

## Example: damage zone

Attach to a hazard with a Collider2D whose Is Trigger box is enabled.

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class DamageZone2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField, Min(0)] private int damage = 1;

    private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
    {
        if (!other.CompareTag("Player")) return;

        PlayerHealth health = other.GetComponentInParent<PlayerHealth>();
        if (health != null)
            health.TakeDamage(damage);
    }
}
```

This expects your project to provide a PlayerHealth component with a public TakeDamage(int amount) method. Keep health logic in that component, not in the hazard.

## Debug checklist

- Use Collider2D and Rigidbody2D, not 3D components.
- Verify the trigger checkbox and layer matrix.
- Check whether the callback is OnTriggerEnter2D or OnCollisionEnter2D.
- Confirm the object has the expected tag and component.
- Avoid using string tags for complex systems when a typed component/interface is more appropriate.

# 2D Ground Checks and Layer Masks

Ground checks decide whether a platformer may jump. A single ray from the player's center can miss edges; a small overlap check beneath the feet is often more robust.

## Overlap-box ground check
Attach to a player with Rigidbody2D and Collider2D. Create a child at the feet and assign it to groundCheck. Put solid ground on a Ground layer and include that layer in groundLayers.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class GroundCheck2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform groundCheck;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 checkSize = new Vector2(0.5f, 0.12f);
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask groundLayers;
        public bool IsGrounded { get; private set; }

        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            if (groundCheck == null)
            {
                IsGrounded = false;
                return;
            }
            IsGrounded = Physics2D.OverlapBox(
                groundCheck.position, checkSize, 0f, groundLayers) != null;
        }

        private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
        {
            if (groundCheck == null) return;
            Gizmos.color = Color.yellow;
            Gizmos.DrawWireCube(groundCheck.position, checkSize);
        }
    }

Tune the size so the check overlaps ground but does not extend high enough to treat a wall as ground. Exclude the player layer from the mask.

## LayerMask notes
A LayerMask is a bit mask, not simply a layer index. Use the Inspector to select layer names, or LayerMask.GetMask("Ground") in code. Verify that layer names exist and capitalization matches.

Common mistakes: Ground omitted from the mask, groundCheck embedded too high inside the player collider, check box too large and detecting walls, missing child Transform assignment, or collision settings preventing interaction.

# 2D Melee Attacks and Area Hitboxes

For melee attacks, use a temporary hitbox or an overlap query during the active frames of an attack. Avoid leaving a damage collider active all the time, which can apply repeated hits.

## One-time overlap attack
Attach this script to the player/weapon. Set the attackPoint child Transform and choose the Enemy layer in targetLayers. Trigger Attack from an input action or animation event.

    using System.Collections.Generic;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class MeleeAttack2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform attackPoint;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 attackSize = new Vector2(1f, 0.7f);
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float damage = 20f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask targetLayers;
        private readonly HashSet<Health2D> hitThisSwing = new();

        public void Attack()
        {
            if (attackPoint == null) return;
            hitThisSwing.Clear();

            Collider2D[] hits = Physics2D.OverlapBoxAll(
                attackPoint.position, attackSize, attackPoint.eulerAngles.z, targetLayers);

            foreach (Collider2D hit in hits)
            {
                Health2D health = hit.GetComponentInParent<Health2D>();
                if (health == null || !hitThisSwing.Add(health)) continue;
                health.TakeDamage(damage);
            }
        }

        private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
        {
            if (attackPoint == null) return;
            Gizmos.color = Color.red;
            Gizmos.matrix = Matrix4x4.TRS(
                attackPoint.position, attackPoint.rotation, Vector3.one);
            Gizmos.DrawWireCube(Vector3.zero, attackSize);
        }
    }

This applies damage immediately once per attack call. For animation-timed attacks, call it from an Animation Event placed during the active swing frames, or use an explicit active hitbox window. OverlapBoxAll allocates an array; for frequent attacks, use a NonAlloc query and handle buffer capacity.

## Common mistakes
- Target layer missing from LayerMask.
- Hit applies multiple times because several child colliders belong to one enemy; the HashSet prevents that within one call.
- Animation event method is on a different object than expected.
- The attack box rotates around the wrong pivot.
- Overlap area is checked continuously instead of only during attack frames.

# Physics2D Query Reference

Physics2D queries inspect the 2D physics world. Check the layer mask and trigger settings for every query. A LayerMask filters which collider layers the query can hit.

## Common queries
- Physics2D.Raycast: cast a ray in a direction and get the first hit.
- Physics2D.CircleCast: sweep a circle along a direction.
- Physics2D.BoxCast: sweep a box along a direction.
- Physics2D.OverlapCircle: detect colliders overlapping a circle.
- Physics2D.OverlapBox: detect colliders overlapping a box.
- Physics2D.OverlapArea: detect colliders overlapping an axis-aligned area.
- Physics2D.Linecast: test a line segment.
- Collider2D.OverlapPoint: check whether a point lies inside a collider.

## Raycast example

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Raycast2DExample : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float distance = 3f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask targetLayers;

        private void Update()
        {
            Vector2 origin = transform.position;
            Vector2 direction = transform.right;
            RaycastHit2D hit = Physics2D.Raycast(origin, direction, distance, targetLayers);
            Debug.DrawRay(origin, direction * distance, hit.collider ? Color.green : Color.red);

            if (hit.collider != null)
                Debug.Log($"Hit {hit.collider.name}", hit.collider);
        }
    }

## Query rules
- Use the query overload that matches the intended maximum distance and layer mask.
- Do not use all-layer queries when you already know the target categories.
- A ray starting inside a collider may behave differently from an approach from outside; test origin placement.
- Decide whether triggers should count as hits.
- For multiple hits, use the appropriate array/list-returning overload and size the buffer correctly.
- For non-alloc queries, handle the possibility that the supplied result buffer fills and truncates results.

## Debugging
Use Debug.DrawRay and draw overlap bounds with Gizmos. Confirm physics layers in Project Settings → Physics 2D. A 2D query only sees Collider2D objects, not 3D Collider components.

# 2D Projectiles, Damage, and Hit Detection

## Setup
Create a projectile prefab with a SpriteRenderer, CircleCollider2D (usually Is Trigger), and this script. If using trigger callbacks, ensure the projectile or target setup includes a Rigidbody2D as required by Unity's 2D physics. Assign the prefab to the weapon. Set its layer and verify Physics 2D collision filtering.

## Projectile script
Save as Projectile2D.cs and attach to the projectile prefab.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Projectile2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float speed = 12f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float damage = 10f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0.1f)] private float lifetime = 3f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask hitLayers = ~0;

        private Vector2 direction;
        private GameObject owner;

        public void Initialize(Vector2 travelDirection, GameObject source)
        {
            direction = travelDirection.sqrMagnitude > 0f
                ? travelDirection.normalized
                : Vector2.right;
            owner = source;
            Destroy(gameObject, lifetime);
        }

        private void Update()
        {
            transform.position += (Vector3)(direction * speed * Time.deltaTime);
        }

        private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
        {
            if (owner != null && other.gameObject == owner) return;
            if ((hitLayers.value & (1 << other.gameObject.layer)) == 0) return;

            Health2D health = other.GetComponentInParent<Health2D>();
            if (health != null) health.TakeDamage(damage);
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }

This simple version moves by Transform and may tunnel at high speeds; use Rigidbody2D movement or a cast per step for fast projectiles. The Health2D component must exist on the target or a parent. Be deliberate about whether a projectile should disappear on hitting non-damageable objects.

## Basic firing script
Attach to a weapon object. The Input System callback may call Fire().

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class ProjectileWeapon2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Projectile2D projectilePrefab;
        [SerializeField] private Transform muzzle;
        [SerializeField, Min(0.01f)] private float cooldown = 0.25f;
        private float nextFireTime;

        public void Fire(Vector2 direction)
        {
            if (Time.time < nextFireTime || projectilePrefab == null) return;
            nextFireTime = Time.time + cooldown;
            Transform origin = muzzle != null ? muzzle : transform;
            Projectile2D projectile = Instantiate(
                projectilePrefab, origin.position, Quaternion.identity);
            projectile.Initialize(direction, gameObject);
        }
    }

## Common mistakes
- Prefab reference is missing.
- Projectile collides with its owner due to incorrect owner/layer filtering.
- Trigger callback never runs because the colliders/Rigidbody2D/layers are incompatible.
- Projectile is aimed using local right when the sprite's forward direction is different.
- Destroy is called immediately on non-target scenery when that is not intended.
- Moving very fast projectiles by Transform causes tunneling.

# 2D Physics Queries and Collision Filtering

## Collision Matrix
Open **Project Settings → Physics 2D** and inspect the Layer Collision Matrix. A pair of layers with its box unchecked will not produce normal physics interactions. A GameObject's Tag does not replace its Layer.

## LayerMask
Use a LayerMask to select which colliders a raycast, cast, or overlap query may detect. This is more reliable than finding any hit and then guessing what it represents.

## Common query choices
- `Physics2D.Raycast`: thin line query, useful for aiming or checking distance to a surface.
- `Physics2D.CircleCast`: sweeps a circle; useful for a rounded ground probe.
- `Physics2D.BoxCast`: sweeps a box-shaped volume.
- `Physics2D.OverlapCircle`: checks an area at a single position.
- `Physics2D.OverlapBox`: checks a rectangular area.

## Example: ground overlap

```csharp
[SerializeField] private Transform feet;
[SerializeField] private float radius = 0.12f;
[SerializeField] private LayerMask groundLayers;

private bool IsGrounded()
{
    return feet != null &&
        Physics2D.OverlapCircle(feet.position, radius, groundLayers);
}

private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
{
    if (feet == null) return;
    Gizmos.DrawWireSphere(feet.position, radius);
}
```

## Trigger vs collision
Use a trigger for pickups, proximity zones, checkpoints, and detection volumes where the overlap should not physically block movement. Use a solid collider for walls and floors. Exact callback requirements depend on collider and Rigidbody2D configuration; check the official documentation for the setup used.

## Common failures
- LayerMask does not include the intended ground layer.
- The probe starts above or inside an unexpected collider.
- Collider is disabled or on an inactive GameObject.
- The object is on the wrong Layer despite having the correct Tag.
- Composite Collider 2D is configured without the required Rigidbody2D/participating collider settings.

Reference: [Physics2D API](https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/Physics2D.html)

# 2D Recipe: Viewport Bounds and Object Wrapping

## Convert screen edges to world positions
Viewport coordinates are normalized: bottom-left is (0,0), top-right is (1,1). The camera converts them to world coordinates.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class ViewBounds2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Camera targetCamera;
        public Bounds WorldBounds { get; private set; }

        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (targetCamera == null) targetCamera = Camera.main;
            if (targetCamera == null) return;

            Vector3 bottomLeft = targetCamera.ViewportToWorldPoint(
                new Vector3(0f, 0f, Mathf.Abs(targetCamera.transform.position.z)));
            Vector3 topRight = targetCamera.ViewportToWorldPoint(
                new Vector3(1f, 1f, Mathf.Abs(targetCamera.transform.position.z)));

            WorldBounds = new Bounds(
                (bottomLeft + topRight) * 0.5f,
                topRight - bottomLeft);
        }
    }

This depth calculation assumes a conventional 2D orthographic camera at negative Z with world objects near Z = 0. If your scene uses another plane, calculate the correct camera-space depth instead.

## Wrapping objects
For a simple space game, wrap an object after it fully exits the view. Decide if wrapping uses center position or renderer bounds; center-only wrapping is shorter but may pop at the edge. Use a camera viewport test for each object, or calculate world bounds and reposition across the opposite edge.

## Common mistakes
- ViewportToWorldPoint z is camera-relative depth, not the world Z coordinate.
- Perspective camera depth calculations differ from orthographic 2D conventions.
- The player's collider or sprite appears clipped when wrapping only the center.
- Multiple camera setups or split-screen mean Camera.main is not always the correct camera.

# 2D Recipe: Camera Shake

A screen shake should usually be an additive offset on a dedicated camera rig, not a second script that competes to set the camera's main position. This example uses unscaled time so the shake can run during a pause; change that deliberately if desired.

## Shake component
Attach to a camera rig child, or to a transform whose base position is owned by another camera-follow system. Call Shake from a gameplay event.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class CameraShake2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float defaultDuration = 0.15f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float defaultMagnitude = 0.15f;
        private Vector3 baseLocalPosition;
        private Coroutine activeShake;

        private void Awake() => baseLocalPosition = transform.localPosition;

        public void Shake()
        {
            Shake(defaultDuration, defaultMagnitude);
        }

        public void Shake(float duration, float magnitude)
        {
            if (activeShake != null) StopCoroutine(activeShake);
            activeShake = StartCoroutine(ShakeRoutine(duration, magnitude));
        }

        private IEnumerator ShakeRoutine(float duration, float magnitude)
        {
            float elapsed = 0f;
            while (elapsed < duration)
            {
                Vector2 offset = Random.insideUnitCircle * magnitude;
                transform.localPosition = baseLocalPosition + new Vector3(offset.x, offset.y, 0f);
                elapsed += Time.unscaledDeltaTime;
                yield return null;
            }
            transform.localPosition = baseLocalPosition;
            activeShake = null;
        }

        private void OnDisable()
        {
            if (activeShake != null) StopCoroutine(activeShake);
            transform.localPosition = baseLocalPosition;
            activeShake = null;
        }
    }

This component assumes the base local position stays fixed. For a camera that follows a moving target, place the shake offset on a dedicated child rig or integrate shake into the camera controller so follow updates don't overwrite it.

## Common mistakes
- Camera follow and shake scripts both write the same Transform.
- Shake routine ends without restoring the original position.
- Shake coroutine is disabled while active and leaves a residual offset.
- Magnitude is too high and makes the game difficult to play.

# 2D Parallax Background

Parallax creates perceived depth by moving background layers at a fraction of camera motion. Place far layers behind nearer layers and assign each a different factor.

## Parallax component
Save as ParallaxLayer2D.cs. Attach to a background layer Transform and assign the camera. Use small factors for distant layers; factors above 1 create faster foreground motion.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class ParallaxLayer2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform cameraTransform;
        [SerializeField, Range(-1f, 1f)] private float factor = 0.3f;
        private Vector3 previousCameraPosition;

        private void Start()
        {
            if (cameraTransform == null && Camera.main != null)
                cameraTransform = Camera.main.transform;
            if (cameraTransform != null)
                previousCameraPosition = cameraTransform.position;
        }

        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (cameraTransform == null) return;
            Vector3 delta = cameraTransform.position - previousCameraPosition;
            transform.position += new Vector3(delta.x * factor, delta.y * factor, 0f);
            previousCameraPosition = cameraTransform.position;
        }
    }

## Tiling
For an infinitely repeating background, use a tiled sprite/material or reposition segments when they pass the camera bounds. Calculate segment width in world units (sprite pixels divided by Pixels Per Unit) and account for camera view width. Avoid moving the same background from multiple scripts.

## Common mistakes
- Camera reference is missing or Camera.main cannot find a tagged MainCamera.
- Background is moved by both parallax and another script.
- Factor is applied to absolute camera position each frame instead of camera delta, causing drift.
- Sprite seams appear due to non-tileable art, filtering, or fractional scaling.
- Background covers gameplay because its Z/sorting order is wrong.

# 2D Combat and Projectiles

Unity 6 uses Rigidbody2D, Collider2D, and Physics2D. This guide provides a shared damage contract, health, melee overlap checks, and projectiles.

## Damage contract — IDamageable.cs

~~~csharp
using UnityEngine;
public interface IDamageable
{
    void TakeDamage(float amount, Vector2 point, Vector2 impulse);
}
~~~

## Health — Health2D.cs

~~~csharp
using System;
using UnityEngine;

[RequireComponent(typeof(Collider2D))]
public class Health2D : MonoBehaviour, IDamageable
{
    [SerializeField, Min(1)] private float maxHealth = 5;
    [SerializeField] private Rigidbody2D body;
    public float Current { get; private set; }
    public float Max => maxHealth;
    public event Action<float, float> Changed;
    public event Action Died;
    private bool dead;

    private void Awake()
    {
        if (!body) body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
        Current = maxHealth;
        Changed?.Invoke(Current, maxHealth);
    }

    public void TakeDamage(float amount, Vector2 point, Vector2 impulse)
    {
        if (dead || amount <= 0) return;
        Current = Mathf.Max(0, Current - amount);
        if (body && body.bodyType == RigidbodyType2D.Dynamic)
            body.AddForce(impulse, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
        Changed?.Invoke(Current, maxHealth);
        if (Current <= 0)
        {
            dead = true;
            Died?.Invoke();
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }

    public void Heal(float amount)
    {
        if (dead || amount <= 0) return;
        Current = Mathf.Min(maxHealth, Current + amount);
        Changed?.Invoke(Current, maxHealth);
    }
}
~~~

## Melee — MeleeAttack2D.cs

~~~csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class MeleeAttack2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private Transform origin;
    [SerializeField] private float radius = 0.7f, damage = 1, knockback = 3;
    [SerializeField] private LayerMask targets;

    public void Attack()
    {
        if (!origin) return;
        var alreadyHit = new HashSet<IDamageable>();
        foreach (Collider2D hit in Physics2D.OverlapCircleAll(origin.position, radius, targets))
        {
            if (hit.transform.root == transform.root) continue;
            IDamageable target = hit.GetComponentInParent<IDamageable>();
            if (target == null || !alreadyHit.Add(target)) continue;
            Vector2 dir = ((Vector2)hit.bounds.center - (Vector2)origin.position).normalized;
            target.TakeDamage(damage, hit.ClosestPoint(origin.position), dir * knockback);
        }
    }

    private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
    {
        if (origin) Gizmos.DrawWireSphere(origin.position, radius);
    }
}
~~~

Call Attack once from an input action or animation event, not every frame while a key is held.

## Projectile — Projectile2D.cs

~~~csharp
using UnityEngine;

[RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D), typeof(Collider2D))]
public class Projectile2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float lifetime = 4;
    private Rigidbody2D body;
    private Transform owner;
    private Vector2 direction;
    private float damage, knockback;
    private bool spent;

    private void Awake()
    {
        body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
        GetComponent<Collider2D>().isTrigger = true;
    }

    public void Launch(Transform source, Vector2 aim, float speed, float damageValue, float force)
    {
        owner = source; direction = aim.normalized;
        damage = damageValue; knockback = force;
        body.gravityScale = 0;
        body.linearVelocity = direction * speed;
        Destroy(gameObject, lifetime);
    }

    private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
    {
        if (spent || (owner && other.transform.root == owner.root)) return;
        IDamageable target = other.GetComponentInParent<IDamageable>();
        if (target == null) return;
        spent = true;
        target.TakeDamage(damage, other.ClosestPoint(transform.position), direction * knockback);
        Destroy(gameObject);
    }
}
~~~

## Launcher — ProjectileLauncher2D.cs

~~~csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class ProjectileLauncher2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private Projectile2D prefab;
    [SerializeField] private Transform muzzle;
    [SerializeField] private float speed = 12, damage = 1, knockback = 2;

    public void Fire(Vector2 direction)
    {
        if (!prefab || !muzzle || direction.sqrMagnitude < 0.001f) return;
        Projectile2D shot = Instantiate(prefab, muzzle.position, Quaternion.identity);
        shot.Launch(transform, direction, speed, damage, knockback);
    }
}
~~~

### Setup and debugging
- Projectile prefab: SpriteRenderer, Rigidbody2D (Gravity Scale 0; Collision Detection Continuous), CircleCollider2D (Is Trigger), Projectile2D.
- Assign prefab and muzzle to launcher. The target needs a Collider2D and a component implementing IDamageable.
- Put targets on a layer included in the melee LayerMask. Configure Physics 2D layer interactions.
- Trigger callbacks require a Rigidbody2D in the pair.
- This projectile stops on damageable objects; add explicit wall handling if needed.
- Unity 6 uses Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity; older versions may expose velocity instead.

# 2D Enemy AI: Patrol, Chase, Attack, Return

This example is a small finite-state machine for simple top-down enemies. It moves directly through the world; it is not pathfinding and will not route around walls.

## EnemyAI2D.cs

~~~csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class EnemyAI2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    private enum State { Patrol, Chase, Attack, Return }
    [SerializeField] private Transform player, pointA, pointB;
    [SerializeField] private MeleeAttack2D attack;
    [SerializeField] private float patrolSpeed = 1.5f, chaseSpeed = 3;
    [SerializeField] private float detectionRange = 5, attackRange = 1, cooldown = 1;
    private Transform patrolTarget;
    private State state = State.Patrol;
    private float nextAttack;

    private void Start()
    {
        if (!player)
        {
            GameObject found = GameObject.FindGameObjectWithTag("Player");
            if (found) player = found.transform;
        }
        patrolTarget = pointA;
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        if (!player || !pointA || !pointB) return;
        float distance = Vector2.Distance(transform.position, player.position);
        if (distance <= attackRange) state = State.Attack;
        else if (distance <= detectionRange) state = State.Chase;
        else if (state == State.Chase || state == State.Attack) state = State.Return;

        if (state == State.Attack)
        {
            if (Time.time >= nextAttack && attack)
            {
                attack.Attack();
                nextAttack = Time.time + cooldown;
            }
            return;
        }

        Transform destination = state == State.Chase ? player : patrolTarget;
        float speed = state == State.Chase ? chaseSpeed : patrolSpeed;
        Vector2 next = Vector2.MoveTowards(transform.position, destination.position, speed * Time.deltaTime);
        transform.position = new Vector3(next.x, next.y, transform.position.z);

        if (state == State.Patrol && Vector2.Distance(transform.position, patrolTarget.position) < 0.12f)
            patrolTarget = patrolTarget == pointA ? pointB : pointA;
        if (state == State.Return && Vector2.Distance(transform.position, patrolTarget.position) < 0.12f)
            state = State.Patrol;
    }
}
~~~

### Setup and upgrades
Enemy needs this script and MeleeAttack2D. Assign player, two patrol points, and attack component. Player needs tag Player. The attack origin and target layer mask must be configured.

- This simple version uses Transform movement. For dynamic Rigidbody2D enemies, move the body in FixedUpdate using MovePosition instead.
- Add Physics2D.Linecast for line-of-sight so walls block detection.
- Add attack wind-up/recovery, animation events, and a dead state.
- For platformers, add ledge checks and jump planning or use a platformer pathfinding solution.

