# 3D AI: Line of Sight and Aggro Range

Distance detects nearby targets, but does not tell whether walls block visibility. Combine a range check with a raycast from an eye point toward the target.

## Detection method
Use this component as a detection helper on an enemy. Assign the eye point and target Transform. The target must be on a layer included in sightLayers if you want the ray to confirm it directly.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class LineOfSight3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform eyePoint;
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float viewRange = 10f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask sightLayers = ~0;

        public bool CanSeeTarget()
        {
            if (eyePoint == null || target == null) return false;
            Vector3 offset = target.position - eyePoint.position;
            float distance = offset.magnitude;
            if (distance > viewRange || distance < 0.001f) return false;

            if (Physics.Raycast(eyePoint.position, offset / distance, out RaycastHit hit,
                    distance, sightLayers, QueryTriggerInteraction.Ignore))
            {
                return hit.transform == target || hit.transform.IsChildOf(target);
            }
            return false;
        }

        private void OnDrawGizmosSelected()
        {
            if (eyePoint == null) return;
            Gizmos.color = Color.yellow;
            Gizmos.DrawWireSphere(eyePoint.position, viewRange);
            if (target != null)
                Gizmos.DrawLine(eyePoint.position, target.position);
        }
    }

If your target has multiple child colliders or a root Rigidbody, test the hierarchy logic against the actual target. A line of sight check is an input to AI state; it should not directly decide every attack/chase behavior.

## Common mistakes
- The ray hits the enemy's own collider first.
- Sight layer mask excludes the target.
- Ray origin is inside a wall or character collider.
- Range check uses inconsistent units.
- Detection is called every frame even when an interval would be sufficient.

# 3D AI: Patrol and Target Detection

This example patrols between assigned NavMesh waypoints and chases a target within a simple distance threshold. It requires the AI Navigation setup, baked NavMesh, NavMeshAgent, and explicit waypoints.

## Patrol/chase agent
Save as PatrolChaseAgent.cs and attach beside NavMeshAgent.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.AI;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(NavMeshAgent))]
    public sealed class PatrolChaseAgent : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform[] patrolPoints;
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float detectionRange = 8f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0.1f)] private float destinationRefresh = 0.2f;
        private NavMeshAgent agent;
        private int pointIndex;
        private float nextRefresh;
        private bool chasing;

        private void Awake() => agent = GetComponent<NavMeshAgent>();

        private void Update()
        {
            if (!agent.isOnNavMesh) return;

            bool canSeeTarget = target != null &&
                (target.position - transform.position).sqrMagnitude <= detectionRange * detectionRange;
            if (canSeeTarget != chasing)
            {
                chasing = canSeeTarget;
                nextRefresh = 0f;
            }

            if (Time.time < nextRefresh) return;
            nextRefresh = Time.time + destinationRefresh;

            if (chasing)
            {
                agent.SetDestination(target.position);
                return;
            }

            if (patrolPoints == null || patrolPoints.Length == 0) return;
            Transform point = patrolPoints[pointIndex];
            if (point == null) return;

            if (!agent.pathPending && agent.remainingDistance <= agent.stoppingDistance + 0.15f)
                pointIndex = (pointIndex + 1) % patrolPoints.Length;

            if (patrolPoints[pointIndex] != null)
                agent.SetDestination(patrolPoints[pointIndex].position);
        }
    }

This uses distance only, not line of sight. Add a raycast between enemy and target if walls should block detection. Do not use a zero stopping distance if the agent continually overshoots narrow waypoints; tune agent radius, speed, acceleration, and stopping distance.

## Common mistakes
- NavMesh has not been baked or agent starts off the mesh.
- A waypoint is null.
- Target detection ignores walls because no line-of-sight test exists.
- Agent destination is changed every frame without a reason.
- Another script drives the same Transform while NavMeshAgent is active.

# 3D AI: State Machines and NavMesh Basics

## State machines
Represent behavior explicitly, such as Patrol → Chase → Attack → Return. Each state defines behavior and transition conditions. This is easier to debug than many overlapping booleans.

## NavMesh setup
1. Install the AI Navigation package compatible with the Unity version.
2. Add a NavMeshSurface to the level and configure included layers.
3. Bake the NavMesh.
4. Add NavMeshAgent to the enemy and ensure it begins on the NavMesh.
5. Assign the target Transform.

## Minimal chase agent
Save as NavMeshChase.cs and attach beside NavMeshAgent.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.AI;
    [RequireComponent(typeof(NavMeshAgent))]
    public sealed class NavMeshChase : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float updateInterval = 0.2f;
        private NavMeshAgent agent;
        private float nextUpdate;
        private void Awake() => agent = GetComponent<NavMeshAgent>();
        private void Update()
        {
            if (target == null || !agent.isOnNavMesh) return;
            if (Time.time < nextUpdate) return;
            nextUpdate = Time.time + updateInterval;
            agent.SetDestination(target.position);
        }
    }

This is path-following, not complete AI. Add detection range, line of sight, attack cooldown, loss-of-target behavior, and explicit transitions.

Common mistakes: NavMesh not baked, agent outside it, bad radius/height, missing target, dynamic obstacles not configured, or multiple systems writing destination/Transform.

# 3D Animation Events

Animation Events call a method at a specific frame/time in an Animation Clip. They are useful for footsteps, weapon hit windows, reload stages, and other moments tightly synchronized to a clip.

## Workflow
1. Select a clip and open the Animation window.
2. Move the playhead to the desired frame and add an Event.
3. Choose a method available on the animated GameObject or its relevant receiver setup.
4. Ensure the method name and supported parameter type match the event.
5. Test transitions, interruptions, and looping.

## Example receiver
Attach to the GameObject that receives the Animation Event.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class AnimationEventReceiver : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public void PlayFootstep()
        {
            // Request a footstep sound through the project's audio system.
        }

        public void OpenDamageWindow()
        {
            // Enable the weapon hitbox or record the start of an attack window.
        }

        public void CloseDamageWindow()
        {
            // Disable the weapon hitbox or record the end of the attack window.
        }

        public void AnimationFinished()
        {
            // Notify gameplay that this clip reached a designated event.
        }
    }

These methods are event entry points; connect them to actual project systems. Avoid putting critical one-time game logic only in a clip event if the animation can be interrupted or skipped. Pair animation timing with gameplay state validation.

## Common failures
- Method exists on another GameObject and the expected receiver cannot find it.
- Method is private/incompatible with the event binding behavior.
- Clip/Animator uses a different asset than the one edited.
- Interrupted animation leaves a hitbox enabled because the closing event never runs.
- Event duplicates damage because hit tracking isn't reset per attack.

# Animator Parameters and Transitions

## Typical state-machine design
A character may have Idle, Walk/Run, Jump, Fall, Land, and Attack states. Avoid creating a separate state for every tiny variation if a Blend Tree or parameter can express it more clearly.

## Parameters
- **Float:** continuous value such as speed.
- **Int:** discrete selection such as weapon type.
- **Bool:** persistent condition such as grounded.
- **Trigger:** one-shot request such as attack.

Names and types must match exactly between the Animator Controller and script.

## Example: drive a speed parameter

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class AnimatorSpeedDriver : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private Animator animator;
    [SerializeField] private Rigidbody body;
    private static readonly int SpeedId = Animator.StringToHash("Speed");

    private void Awake()
    {
        if (animator == null) animator = GetComponentInChildren<Animator>();
        if (body == null) body = GetComponent<Rigidbody>();
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        if (animator == null || body == null) return;
        Vector3 horizontalVelocity = body.linearVelocity;
        horizontalVelocity.y = 0f;
        animator.SetFloat(SpeedId, horizontalVelocity.magnitude);
    }
}
```

This example uses the Unity 6 Rigidbody.linearVelocity API. Check the version-specific API if you use an older Editor. For a CharacterController, derive speed from the controller's movement displacement or a movement variable instead.

## Transition settings
- **Conditions:** parameters required to enter the destination state.
- **Has Exit Time:** wait for a normalized point in the current animation before transitioning.
- **Transition Duration:** blends outgoing and incoming motion.
- **Interruption Source:** controls whether and how another transition can interrupt.
- **Layer Weight / Avatar Mask:** affect layered animation.

## Debugging checklist
1. Is the correct Animator Controller assigned?
2. Are parameter names and types identical?
3. Does the transition condition ever become true?
4. Is Exit Time delaying the transition?
5. Is the Animator on the expected layer and active?
6. Is root motion enabled while script movement also changes position?

Reference: [Animator API](https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/Animator.html)

# 3D Animator Blend Trees and Locomotion

A Blend Tree blends motion clips using parameter values instead of many separate transitions. A common locomotion setup blends idle, walk, and run based on Speed. A 2D directional Blend Tree can blend forward/back/strafe clips by horizontal and vertical movement values.

## Setup for 1D locomotion
1. Create an Animator Controller and add a Blend Tree state.
2. Choose a 1D Blend Tree and use a Float parameter named Speed.
3. Add idle, walk, and run clips with thresholds appropriate to your Speed units.
4. Ensure looping is configured on locomotion clips.
5. Drive the parameter from controller velocity or normalized input.

## Parameter driver
Attach to the object containing the Animator. Assign Animator and the Transform/Rigidbody from which speed is measured; this example measures Rigidbody velocity in Unity 6.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class LocomotionBlendDriver : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Animator animator;
        [SerializeField] private Rigidbody body;
        [SerializeField, Min(0.01f)] private float speedDampTime = 0.1f;
        private static readonly int SpeedId = Animator.StringToHash("Speed");

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (animator == null) animator = GetComponent<Animator>();
            if (body == null) body = GetComponentInParent<Rigidbody>();
        }

        private void Update()
        {
            if (animator == null || body == null) return;
            Vector3 horizontal = body.linearVelocity;
            horizontal.y = 0f;
            animator.SetFloat(SpeedId, horizontal.magnitude, speedDampTime, Time.deltaTime);
        }
    }

The animation's thresholds must match the units being supplied. If the Rigidbody's velocity is measured in world units per second, thresholds must use comparable values. If using root motion, choose one authority for movement and avoid simultaneous scripted motion.

## Common mistakes
- Parameter name differs from the controller.
- Speed values do not match Blend Tree thresholds.
- Idle clip has an unintended root displacement.
- Blend parameter is fed movement input magnitude but thresholds assume world speed.
- Animator is on a child but code looks only on the root.

# 3D Animation: Root Motion vs Code-Driven Movement

## Two movement authorities
- **Code-driven movement:** a controller moves the character; animation follows the movement state. This is predictable for platformers and responsive controls.
- **Root motion:** animation clip displacement moves the character. This can produce natural authored movement, but responsiveness and network/physics integration need care.

Do not let Animator root motion and a controller independently move the same Transform unless you deliberately combine them.

## Animator settings
1. Add Animator and assign the controller.
2. Inspect the Avatar and Apply Root Motion option.
3. Check animation import settings for root transform rotation/position baking.
4. Test transition blending and loop behavior.
5. Decide which component owns position and rotation.

## Inspect root motion
This script logs root motion delta for diagnosis. Attach to the same object as Animator.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class RootMotionInspector : MonoBehaviour
    {
        private void OnAnimatorMove()
        {
            Animator animator = GetComponent<Animator>();
            if (animator == null) return;
            Debug.DrawRay(transform.position, animator.deltaPosition, Color.cyan);
        }
    }

For actual root-motion application, implement OnAnimatorMove intentionally and consider how the CharacterController or Rigidbody should be moved. The diagnostic snippet is not a complete root-motion controller.

## Common problems
- Apply Root Motion enabled while controller also moves the same object.
- Root transforms are baked incorrectly in import settings.
- Animator transitions interrupt locomotion clips.
- Root motion moves the root but collider/controller remains misconfigured.

# 3D Spatial Audio

## Setup
Keep one active AudioListener, typically on the player camera. Add an AudioSource to the sound-producing object, assign a clip, and disable Play On Awake for code-triggered playback. Set Spatial Blend toward 1 for 3D positioning. Configure Min Distance, Max Distance, and rolloff for the scale of the world.

## Sound emitter
Save as SpatialSoundEmitter.cs and attach beside an AudioSource.

    using UnityEngine;
    [RequireComponent(typeof(AudioSource))]
    public sealed class SpatialSoundEmitter : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private AudioClip clip;
        [SerializeField, Range(0f, 1f)] private float volume = 1f;
        private AudioSource source;
