# 3D Third-Person Camera Collision and Smoothing

A third-person camera should not clip through walls. Choose a target pivot near the character and a desired offset behind it. Cast from the pivot toward that offset, excluding the player layer when appropriate.

## Collision distance example
Attach to a camera rig. Assign targetPivot, and set the local desired camera offset. The script moves the rig's child camera along the vector between pivot and desired position.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class ThirdPersonCameraCollision : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform targetPivot;
        [SerializeField] private Transform cameraTransform;
        [SerializeField] private Vector3 localOffset = new Vector3(0f, 1f, -4f);
        [SerializeField, Min(0.01f)] private float sphereRadius = 0.2f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float padding = 0.1f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask obstacleLayers = ~0;

        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (targetPivot == null || cameraTransform == null) return;

            Vector3 desired = targetPivot.TransformPoint(localOffset);
            Vector3 direction = desired - targetPivot.position;
            float distance = direction.magnitude;
            if (distance < 0.001f) return;
            direction /= distance;

            float allowedDistance = distance;
            if (Physics.SphereCast(targetPivot.position, sphereRadius, direction,
                    out RaycastHit hit, distance, obstacleLayers,
                    QueryTriggerInteraction.Ignore))
                allowedDistance = Mathf.Max(0.05f, hit.distance - padding);

            cameraTransform.position = targetPivot.position + direction * allowedDistance;
            cameraTransform.rotation = Quaternion.LookRotation(
                targetPivot.position - cameraTransform.position, Vector3.up);
        }
    }

This is a basic collision solver, not a replacement for Cinemachine's camera collision extension. Ensure obstacleLayers excludes the player and camera target. For smoothing, filter the allowed distance carefully so the camera moves inward quickly when blocked but returns outward without lagging too much.

## Common mistakes
- SphereCast hits the player's own collider.
- Offset uses the wrong local axis for the camera rig.
- Camera becomes too close to the pivot at a wall and clips into the player.
- Camera follow and this collision script write the same position independently.

# 3D Camera: First- and Third-Person Foundations

For first person, parent the camera to a pivot at eye height. For third person, follow a pivot near shoulder height. Separate character yaw from camera pitch: yaw rotates the player around world Y, while pitch rotates a child pivot around local X.

## Mouse look
Save as MouseLook3D.cs. Attach to the player root and assign a **separate child Transform** as pitchPivot. This script requires the Input System package.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;

    public sealed class MouseLook3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform pitchPivot;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float sensitivity = 0.08f;
        [SerializeField] private float minPitch = -80f;
        [SerializeField] private float maxPitch = 80f;
        private float pitch;

        private void Start()
        {
            if (pitchPivot == null || pitchPivot == transform)
            {
                Debug.LogError("Assign a separate child camera pitch pivot.", this);
                enabled = false;
                return;
            }

            pitch = pitchPivot.localEulerAngles.x;
            if (pitch > 180f) pitch -= 360f;
            LockCursor(true);
        }

        private void Update()
        {
            if (Mouse.current != null && Cursor.lockState == CursorLockMode.Locked)
            {
                Vector2 delta = Mouse.current.delta.ReadValue() * sensitivity;
                transform.Rotate(Vector3.up, delta.x, Space.World);
                pitch = Mathf.Clamp(pitch - delta.y, minPitch, maxPitch);
                pitchPivot.localRotation = Quaternion.Euler(pitch, 0f, 0f);
            }

            if (Keyboard.current != null && Keyboard.current.escapeKey.wasPressedThisFrame)
                LockCursor(false);
            if (Mouse.current != null && Mouse.current.leftButton.wasPressedThisFrame)
                LockCursor(true);
        }

        private static void LockCursor(bool locked)
        {
            Cursor.lockState = locked ? CursorLockMode.Locked : CursorLockMode.None;
            Cursor.visible = !locked;
        }

        private void OnDisable() => LockCursor(false);
    }

The root handles yaw; the child pivot handles pitch. The camera should be a child of pitchPivot or attached beneath it. This is mouse-look only, not a full character controller. Gamepad look needs stick handling. Integrate cursor behavior with pause/settings menus rather than allowing two systems to fight over cursor state.

## Third-person cameras
A third-person camera needs wall collision. Use Cinemachine follow/collider features when available, or cast from the target pivot toward the desired camera position and shorten the distance to obstacles.

## Common mistakes
- pitchPivot is the same Transform as the yaw root.
- A camera and player rotate on all axes together.
- Cursor remains locked while a menu is open.
- Pitch is not clamped.
- Camera clips through walls or player geometry.

# Coroutines, Events, and Asynchronous Work

## Coroutines
Coroutines spread a sequence across frames. They are not background threads. Use them for cooldowns, timed effects, and waiting for Unity conditions.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class CooldownExample : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float cooldownSeconds = 1f;
        private bool ready = true;
        public void TryUse()
        {
            if (!ready) return;
            ready = false;
            StartCoroutine(ResetCooldown());
        }
        private IEnumerator ResetCooldown()
        {
            yield return new WaitForSeconds(cooldownSeconds);
            ready = true;
        }
    }

WaitForSeconds is affected by Time.timeScale; WaitForSecondsRealtime is not. Coroutines do not parallelize CPU-heavy code, and lifetime depends on the hosting component/GameObject.

## Events
Events let one system notify listeners without directly depending on each listener. A health component can raise Died and UI/audio/game flow can respond. Subscribe in OnEnable and unsubscribe in OnDisable when re-enable cycles are possible.

    using System;
    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class EventPublisher : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public event Action Changed;
        public void NotifyChanged() => Changed?.Invoke();
    }

Ensure subscribers unsubscribe and event order is deliberate. Avoid retaining stale scene references.

## Async and threads
Use async/await when the Unity API or package supports asynchronous operations. Most UnityEngine object APIs must be used on the main thread. Do not call Transform, GameObject, or other engine APIs from Task.Run worker threads. Cancellation and object lifetime matter across scene changes.

Choose Update for frame logic, FixedUpdate for physics, coroutines for timed main-loop sequences, events for notifications, async/await for supported asynchronous operations, and Jobs/threads only when data/API constraints permit.

# C# Events and Interfaces in Unity

Events let a component announce that something happened. Interfaces define a contract shared by different components. Both reduce unnecessary direct dependencies when used carefully.

## Event publisher
Save as EventChannelExample.cs.

    using System;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class EventChannelExample : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public event Action<int> ScoreChanged;

        public void SetScore(int score)
        {
            ScoreChanged?.Invoke(score);
        }
    }

A UI component can subscribe in OnEnable and unsubscribe in OnDisable. If it enables after the last change, it must also obtain the current state separately; events notify changes, not a guaranteed history.

## Interface contract

    public interface IDamageable
    {
        void TakeDamage(float amount);
    }

An enemy Health component and a destructible crate can both implement IDamageable. A weapon then calls the interface rather than requiring one concrete Health class. To look it up from a Collider, obtain MonoBehaviours from the collider or its parents and check which implement the interface.

## Subscription pattern

    private void OnEnable()
    {
        if (publisher != null) publisher.ScoreChanged += OnScoreChanged;
    }

    private void OnDisable()
    {
        if (publisher != null) publisher.ScoreChanged -= OnScoreChanged;
    }

Use the actual publisher field and a method with a matching signature in your component. Avoid adding anonymous event lambdas repeatedly if they cannot later be unsubscribed.

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting to unsubscribe creates duplicate callbacks or stale references.
- Expecting a late subscriber to receive earlier events.
- Event publisher is destroyed before the subscriber cleans up.
- Interface reference is not found because only the hit child component was checked.
- Event code silently assumes a specific script execution order.

# 3D Math and Quaternion Recipes

## Rotate toward a target
Compute direction from the current position to the target, then create a target rotation. Handle the zero direction before calling LookRotation.

    Vector3 direction = target.position - transform.position;
    if (direction.sqrMagnitude > 0.0001f)
    {
        Quaternion targetRotation = Quaternion.LookRotation(direction, Vector3.up);
        transform.rotation = Quaternion.RotateTowards(
            transform.rotation, targetRotation, turnSpeed * Time.deltaTime);
    }

For Rigidbody rotation, use the Rigidbody API at the appropriate physics time rather than directly assigning Transform rotation.

## Angles
- Vector3.Angle(a, b): unsigned angle between two vectors.
- Mathf.DeltaAngle(current, target): shortest signed difference between angles.
- Mathf.Atan2(y, x): angle from two coordinates.
- Quaternion.Euler(x, y, z): construct a rotation from Euler angles.
- Quaternion.Inverse(q): inverse rotation.
- Quaternion.RotateTowards(a, b, maxDegreesDelta): bounded rotation step.

Euler angles are convenient to inspect but can wrap around and lead to unintuitive interpolation. Quaternions are the usual representation for interpolating rotations.

## Convert between spaces

    Vector3 worldPoint = transform.TransformPoint(localPoint);
    Vector3 localPoint = transform.InverseTransformPoint(worldPoint);
    Vector3 worldDirection = transform.TransformDirection(localDirection);
    Vector3 localDirection = transform.InverseTransformDirection(worldDirection);

Point conversions include position and scale; direction conversions are for orientation/direction and differ from point conversions.

## Screen and world
Camera.ScreenToWorldPoint converts a screen point using camera-space depth; its input z is a distance from the camera, not the desired world Z. Camera.WorldToScreenPoint converts a world point to pixels. Camera.ScreenPointToRay creates a world ray through a screen pixel; use ViewportPointToRay for normalized viewport coordinates.

## Common mistakes
- Confusing local and world coordinate values.
- Assuming ScreenToWorldPoint input z is a world-space Z.
- Applying yaw/pitch on the wrong Transform.
- Interpolating Euler angles directly when an angular wrap occurs.
- Calling LookRotation with a zero direction.

# 3D Cooldowns, Timers, and Temporary Effects

## Cooldown pattern
Use a timestamp for a simple cooldown rather than a coroutine if you only need to check whether an action is ready.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class CooldownGate : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float cooldown = 1f;
        private float nextAllowedTime;

        public bool TryUse()
        {
            if (Time.time < nextAllowedTime) return false;
            nextAllowedTime = Time.time + cooldown;
            return true;
        }

This cooldown uses scaled game time, so it pauses at timeScale zero. Use Time.unscaledTime if it must continue during pause.

## Temporary effect with coroutine
Use a Coroutine when an effect needs a start, wait, and cleanup sequence. Ensure cleanup happens if the object is disabled/destroyed; for robust effects, centralize their ownership and cancellation.

## Timer choices
- Time.time / Time.deltaTime: scaled gameplay time.
- Time.unscaledTime / Time.unscaledDeltaTime: real elapsed application time unaffected by timeScale.
- WaitForSeconds: scaled coroutine delay.
- WaitForSecondsRealtime: unscaled coroutine delay.
- FixedUpdate / fixedDeltaTime: physics-step timing.

## Common mistakes
- Cooldown initialized to zero but current scaled time assumptions differ.
- Timer is reset by every repeated input event.
- Pause state stops a timer that was meant to be real-time.
- Multiple scripts independently control the same temporary state.

# 3D Doors, Buttons, and Interaction

This is a component-based interaction pattern. The raycast controller detects what the camera points at; the target implements an interactable contract.

## Interface

    public interface IInteractable
    {
        string Prompt { get; }
        void Interact(GameObject interactor);
    }

Save this as IInteractable.cs. Add a component to the door that implements it.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class SimpleDoor : MonoBehaviour, IInteractable
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform hinge;
        [SerializeField] private Vector3 openLocalEuler = new Vector3(0f, 90f, 0f);
        [SerializeField] private Vector3 closedLocalEuler;
        private bool isOpen;

        public string Prompt => isOpen ? "Close door" : "Open door";

        public void Interact(GameObject interactor)
        {
            if (hinge == null) return;
            isOpen = !isOpen;
            hinge.localRotation = Quaternion.Euler(isOpen ? openLocalEuler : closedLocalEuler);
        }
    }

Create an empty hinge pivot at the door's hinge edge, parent the door mesh under it, and assign Hinge. Ensure the local Euler values suit the model's orientation. This immediate rotation is a prototype; use a Coroutine, Animator, or tweening package for an animated door.

## Raycast detector
A camera script can raycast from its center, get the hit component (or a parent component), then call Interact when the input action is triggered. Configure a range and layer mask; don't let UI prompts independently perform the interaction.

## Common mistakes
- Pivot is at the center instead of the hinge edge.
- Mesh/model local axes differ from assumed opening direction.
- Interface implementation is on a parent but only GetComponent is used.
- Target moves out of range between prompt and input.

# Health and damage system (3D)

This component separates health state from collision, weapons, UI, and animation.

## Health3D.cs

```csharp
using System;
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class Health3D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField, Min(1)] private int maxHealth = 100;
    public int CurrentHealth { get; private set; }
    public int MaxHealth => maxHealth;
    public bool IsDead => CurrentHealth <= 0;

    public event Action<int, int> HealthChanged;
    public event Action Died;

    private void Awake()
    {
        CurrentHealth = maxHealth;
    }

    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        if (IsDead || amount <= 0) return;

        CurrentHealth = Mathf.Max(0, CurrentHealth - amount);
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);

        if (IsDead) Died?.Invoke();
    }

    public void Heal(int amount)
    {
        if (IsDead || amount <= 0) return;

        CurrentHealth = Mathf.Min(maxHealth, CurrentHealth + amount);
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }
}
```

## Integration

Call TakeDamage from a collision, hazard, or gameplay event. Subscribe the UI to HealthChanged and subscribe a game manager to Died. Keep the component that detects an impact separate from the component that owns health.

## Production considerations

- Decide whether damage can be blocked, reduced, or ignored during invulnerability.
- Use damage data/structs if damage types and sources become complex.
- Avoid relying on object names to identify damage recipients.
- For networked games, health changes should be validated by the authoritative server/host.

# 3D Health, Damage, and Death

## Health component
Save as Health3D.cs and attach to any damageable actor. Other scripts call TakeDamage; death effects or respawn behavior can subscribe to Died.

    using System;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Health3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(1f)] private float maxHealth = 100f;
        public float CurrentHealth { get; private set; }
        public float MaxHealth => maxHealth;
        public bool IsDead { get; private set; }
        public event Action<float, float> HealthChanged;
        public event Action Died;

        private void Awake()
        {
            CurrentHealth = maxHealth;
        }

        private void Start()
        {
            HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
        }

        public void TakeDamage(float amount)
        {
            if (IsDead || amount <= 0f) return;

            CurrentHealth = Mathf.Max(0f, CurrentHealth - amount);
            HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);

            if (CurrentHealth <= 0f)
            {
                IsDead = true;
                Died?.Invoke();
            }
        }

        public void Heal(float amount)
        {
            if (IsDead || amount <= 0f) return;
            CurrentHealth = Mathf.Min(maxHealth, CurrentHealth + amount);
            HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
        }
    }

If a UI subscribes in OnEnable before Start, it receives the initial event. If the UI may enable later, read CurrentHealth and MaxHealth immediately after subscribing. Decide deliberately whether healing after death is allowed.

## Damage interface
For interchangeable actors, define an IDamageable interface with TakeDamage(float amount). Weapons can detect this interface, rather than depend on a concrete Health3D class. Unity Inspector serialization of interface references needs a wrapper or UnityEngine.Object reference plus a cast.

## Death flow
Use Died to trigger a death animation, score update, respawn, or destruction. Do not put all of these into Health3D; health owns health state while a game-flow system decides what death means.

## Common mistakes
- Multiple hit callbacks apply repeated damage.
- UI subscribes more than once or fails to unsubscribe.
- Health starts at zero because initialization was skipped.
- HealthChanged is fired but the UI assumes event parameters have a different order.

# 3D Camera Raycast Interaction Controller

This generic interaction controller looks from the center of the camera, finds an interactable component, and calls Interact when a button is pressed. The target script can be a door, switch, terminal, pickup, or other interaction object.

## Interface
Save as IInteractable.cs.

    using UnityEngine;

    public interface IInteractable
    {
        string Prompt { get; }
        void Interact(GameObject interactor);
    }

## Controller
Save as CameraInteractor3D.cs. Requires the Input System package, Camera reference, and an Interact action set up as a Button. Connect the action callback or call TryInteract from your input layer.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class CameraInteractor3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Camera viewCamera;
        [SerializeField, Min(0.1f)] private float range = 3f;
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask layers = ~0;

        public string CurrentPrompt { get; private set; }

        private void Update()
        {
            CurrentPrompt = string.Empty;
            if (viewCamera == null) return;

            Ray ray = viewCamera.ViewportPointToRay(new Vector3(0.5f, 0.5f, 0f));
            if (!Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit, range, layers,
                    QueryTriggerInteraction.Ignore))
                return;

            IInteractable interactable = FindInteractable(hit.collider);
            if (interactable != null) CurrentPrompt = interactable.Prompt;
        }

        public void TryInteract()
        {
            if (viewCamera == null) return;
            Ray ray = viewCamera.ViewportPointToRay(new Vector3(0.5f, 0.5f, 0f));
            if (!Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit, range, layers,
                    QueryTriggerInteraction.Ignore))
                return;

            IInteractable interactable = FindInteractable(hit.collider);
            interactable?.Interact(gameObject);
        }

        private static IInteractable FindInteractable(Collider hit)
        {
            MonoBehaviour[] behaviours = hit.GetComponentsInParent<MonoBehaviour>();
            foreach (MonoBehaviour behaviour in behaviours)
                if (behaviour is IInteractable item) return item;
            return null;
        }
    }

The controller reads the target every Update for a prompt, but rechecks at the moment of interaction so stale targets don't activate. If the interactor should be the player's root rather than the camera/controller object, assign and pass that root explicitly.

## Common mistakes
- Interface component is on a parent, so the code searches only the hit child.
- Target layer is not included in the mask.
- The prompt isn't cleared when nothing is hit.
- Input triggers TryInteract on both press and hold, repeating an action.
- A stale prompt is trusted without rechecking range.

# 3D Inventory and Item Definitions

Separate shared authored item definitions from each player's runtime inventory. ScriptableObjects are useful for definitions; inventory counts are mutable runtime state.

## Item definition
Save as ItemDefinition.cs.

    using UnityEngine;

    [CreateAssetMenu(menuName = "Game/Item Definition")]
    public sealed class ItemDefinition : ScriptableObject
    {
        public string itemId = "item.basic";
        public string displayName = "Basic Item";
        public Sprite icon;
        [TextArea] public string description;
        [Min(1)] public int maxStack = 99;
    }

Create definitions through the Project window's Create menu. Keep itemId unique and stable if inventory data will be saved.

## Runtime inventory
This minimal example supports one count per item definition; it does not implement slots, weight, equipment, or serialization. Add returns the number of items accepted so callers can keep any overflow.

    using System.Collections.Generic;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class SimpleInventory : MonoBehaviour
    {
        private readonly Dictionary<ItemDefinition, int> counts = new();

        public int GetCount(ItemDefinition item)
        {
            return item != null && counts.TryGetValue(item, out int count) ? count : 0;
        }

        public int Add(ItemDefinition item, int amount)
        {
            if (item == null || amount <= 0) return 0;
            int current = GetCount(item);
            int accepted = Mathf.Min(amount, Mathf.Max(0, item.maxStack - current));
            if (accepted > 0) counts[item] = current + accepted;
            return accepted;
        }

        public bool Remove(ItemDefinition item, int amount)
        {
            if (item == null || amount <= 0 || GetCount(item) < amount) return false;
            int remaining = GetCount(item) - amount;
            if (remaining == 0) counts.Remove(item);
            else counts[item] = remaining;
            return true;
        }
    }

If Add returns less than requested amount, keep or drop the overflow according to game rules. Dictionaries are not directly serialized by JsonUtility; save a serializable list of item IDs/counts and resolve IDs to definitions during load.

## Common mistakes
- Item IDs change, so existing saves no longer resolve.
- ScriptableObject assets are mutated as player-owned state.
- Inventory UI becomes the source of truth instead of displaying inventory data.
- Overflow is silently discarded.

# 3D Object Pooling

Pooling reuses instantiated objects instead of repeatedly creating and destroying them. It is useful for bullets, impact effects, collectibles, and other short-lived objects with high spawn rates. It may be unnecessary for infrequent objects; profile first.

## Basic pool
Attach to a manager, assign a prefab and initial count, then call Get to spawn and Release to recycle. This minimal version does not prevent a caller from returning an object twice; add ownership/state tracking for production systems.

    using System.Collections.Generic;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class ObjectPool3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
        [SerializeField, Min(0)] private int initialSize = 10;
        private readonly Queue<GameObject> available = new();

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (prefab == null)
            {
                Debug.LogError("Assign a pool prefab.", this);
                enabled = false;
                return;
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < initialSize; i++) CreateOne();
        }

        private GameObject CreateOne()
        {
            GameObject item = Instantiate(prefab, transform);
            item.SetActive(false);
            available.Enqueue(item);
            return item;
        }

        public GameObject Get(Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)
        {
            if (available.Count == 0) CreateOne();
            GameObject item = available.Dequeue();
            item.transform.SetPositionAndRotation(position, rotation);
            item.SetActive(true);
            return item;
        }

        public void Release(GameObject item)
        {
            if (item == null) return;
            item.SetActive(false);
            item.transform.SetParent(transform);
            available.Enqueue(item);
        }
    }

## Reset on reuse
Pooled objects must reset Rigidbody velocity/angular velocity, lifetime timers, targets, collision flags, particle state, trail renderers, and gameplay state. OnEnable is a common place to start a fresh lifetime, but don't assume every referenced object survives between uses.

## Common mistakes
- Object is released twice and appears twice in the queue.
- Rigidbody keeps velocity from the previous launch.
- Particle/trail renderer retains old visual data.
- Event subscriptions accumulate after repeated enable cycles.
- Pool grows without a maximum or profiling shows pooling was not a bottleneck.

# 3D Pickups and Interactable Objects

## Trigger pickup
Create a prefab with a visible mesh, Collider set to Is Trigger, and this script. The player needs the tag configured in the Inspector.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Pickup3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private string playerTag = "Player";
        [SerializeField] private int value = 1;
        private bool collected;

        private void OnTriggerEnter(Collider other)
        {
            if (collected || !other.CompareTag(playerTag)) return;
            collected = true;

            PlayerInventory inventory = other.GetComponentInParent<PlayerInventory>();
            if (inventory != null) inventory.AddCoins(value);
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }

Minimal inventory example:

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class PlayerInventory : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public int Coins { get; private set; }

        public void AddCoins(int amount)
        {
            if (amount > 0) Coins += amount;
        }
    }

A valid Rigidbody/collider relationship is needed for trigger callbacks. Consider using an explicit interface/event to support multiple actor types without relying on a specific tag.

## Raycast interaction
For doors, switches, and terminals, cast from the center of the camera. If a ray hits an object implementing an interaction component, call its Interact method. Limit range and use an interactable layer mask. Keep interaction logic separate from the UI prompt that says “Press E”.

## Safety for interactions
Validate the target again when the action occurs; do not trust only a stale UI prompt. Clear focus when the object leaves range or is destroyed. Avoid letting a prompt's hidden UI object receive gameplay input accidentally.

## Common mistakes
- Collider isn't a trigger or the Rigidbody setup is invalid.
- Player tag differs from configured tag.
- Inventory reference is on the player root but lookup happens on a child.
- A pickup awards multiple times because there is no collected guard.

# 3D Prefabs and Spawn Systems

Build an object in the scene, add required components, configure collider/Rigidbody settings, test it, then drag the root into the Project window to create a prefab. Use prefab variants for related objects that share a base.

## Spawner
Save as PrefabSpawner3D.cs and assign a prefab and optional spawn point.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class PrefabSpawner3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
        [SerializeField] private Transform spawnPoint;
        public GameObject Spawn()
        {
            if (prefab == null)
            {
                Debug.LogError("PrefabSpawner3D: assign a prefab.", this);
                return null;
            }
            Transform point = spawnPoint != null ? spawnPoint : transform;
            return Instantiate(prefab, point.position, point.rotation);
        }
    }

## Spawn-system considerations
- Validate random spawn positions against bounds and obstacles.
- Spawned Rigidbody objects may need velocity/state reset if pooled.
- Avoid spawning inside walls or the player.
- Bound spawn frequency and maximum active count.
- Use pooling for high-frequency projectiles and effects.
- Avoid scene-instance dependencies on prefab assets; assign scene references at runtime.

# Save simple game data with JSON

Use JSON for small structured settings or progress data. Do not store passwords, secrets, or authoritative multiplayer state in a local save file.

## SaveData.cs

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using UnityEngine;

[Serializable]
public sealed class SaveData
{
    public int level = 1;
    public int coins = 0;
    public float musicVolume = 0.8f;
}

public static class SaveSystem
{
    private static string SavePath =>
        Path.Combine(Application.persistentDataPath, "save.json");

    public static void Save(SaveData data)
    {
        if (data == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(data));

        string json = JsonUtility.ToJson(data, true);
        File.WriteAllText(SavePath, json);
    }

    public static SaveData Load()
    {
        if (!File.Exists(SavePath))
            return new SaveData();

        try
        {
            string json = File.ReadAllText(SavePath);
            SaveData data = JsonUtility.FromJson<SaveData>(json);
            return data ?? new SaveData();
        }
        catch (Exception exception)
        {
            Debug.LogWarning($"Could not load save: {exception.Message}");
            return new SaveData();
        }
    }
}
```

## Usage

Call `SaveSystem.Save(data)` when progress changes or at safe save points. Call `SaveSystem.Load()` at startup. For important saves, consider versioning, backup files, validation, and atomic writes.

## Limitations

Unity's JsonUtility has serialization restrictions, including limited support for dictionaries and polymorphic structures. Use a suitable serializer if your data model requires those features.

# Saving and Loading Small Amounts of Data

This example saves local progress as JSON under Application.persistentDataPath. It is not secure against tampering and should not store secrets or be trusted for competitive authority.

## Save manager
Save as LocalSaveSystem.cs and create one manager object in the first scene.

    using System;
    using System.IO;
    using UnityEngine;
    [Serializable]
    public sealed class SaveData
    {
        public int coins;
        public int highestLevel = 1;
        public float musicVolume = 1f;
    }
    public sealed class LocalSaveSystem : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public static LocalSaveSystem Instance { get; private set; }
        public SaveData Data { get; private set; } = new SaveData();
        private string SavePath => Path.Combine(Application.persistentDataPath, "save.json");
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (Instance != null && Instance != this)
            {
                Destroy(gameObject);
                return;
            }
            Instance = this;
            DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject);
            Load();
        }
        public void Save()
        {
            try
            {
                File.WriteAllText(SavePath, JsonUtility.ToJson(Data, true));
            }
            catch (Exception exception)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Save failed: {exception.Message}", this);
            }
        }
        public void Load()
        {
            if (!File.Exists(SavePath))
            {
                Data = new SaveData();
                return;
            }
            try
            {
                Data = JsonUtility.FromJson<SaveData>(File.ReadAllText(SavePath)) ?? new SaveData();
            }
            catch (Exception exception)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Load failed: {exception.Message}", this);
                Data = new SaveData();
            }
        }
        private void OnApplicationPause(bool paused)
        {
            if (paused) Save();
        }
        private void OnApplicationQuit() => Save();
    }

## Limitations
JsonUtility serializes fields and supported types, not arbitrary properties or dictionaries. Add a save-version field and migration logic when the schema changes. Save at meaningful checkpoints because mobile apps may not receive a reliable quit callback. persistentDataPath differs by platform. Handle I/O errors and invalid save files.

# 3D Scene Loading: Synchronous vs Asynchronous

SceneManager.LoadScene loads a scene by name or build index. LoadSceneAsync is useful when a scene transition could cause a visible stall. All destination scenes must be included in the active Build Profile.

## Asynchronous loader
Save as AsyncSceneLoader.cs and attach it to a manager or transition UI.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;

    public sealed class AsyncSceneLoader : MonoBehaviour
    {
        private bool loading;

        public void LoadScene(string sceneName)
        {
            if (loading || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(sceneName)) return;
            StartCoroutine(LoadRoutine(sceneName));
        }

        private IEnumerator LoadRoutine(string sceneName)
        {
            loading = true;
            AsyncOperation operation = SceneManager.LoadSceneAsync(sceneName);
            if (operation == null)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Could not start loading scene: {sceneName}", this);
                loading = false;
                yield break;
            }

            while (!operation.isDone)
                yield return null;

            loading = false;
        }
    }

Use a loading screen when it helps users understand a longer transition. If setting allowSceneActivation to false, be aware that progress behavior and activation rules differ; explicitly restore/allow activation when ready so loading cannot stall forever.

## Transition design
Decide which scene owns persistent managers, audio, loading UI, and player data. Prevent duplicated DontDestroyOnLoad objects. Restore Time.timeScale before loading a gameplay scene after pause.

## Common mistakes
- Scene not included in the Build Profile.
- Loading called repeatedly by multiple UI events.
- Persistent managers duplicate across scene transitions.
- Async operation is held before activation and never released.
- A loading UI references an object destroyed during the scene change.
Official API: https://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/SceneManagement.SceneManager.LoadSceneAsync.html

# 3D Scene Transitions and Pause

Add every scene to the active Build Profile. Prefer explicit scene names or build indices that are configured intentionally. The example uses Escape and requires the Input System package.

## Pause and restart manager
Save as GameFlow3D.cs. Assign the pause menu object and wire menu buttons to Resume, LoadMenu, or Restart.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;
    using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;

    public sealed class GameFlow3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject pauseMenu;
        [SerializeField] private string menuSceneName = "MainMenu";
        private bool paused;

        private void Start() => SetPaused(false);

        private void Update()
        {
            if (Keyboard.current != null &&
                Keyboard.current.escapeKey.wasPressedThisFrame)
                SetPaused(!paused);
        }

        public void Resume() => SetPaused(false);

        public void Restart()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(SceneManager.GetActiveScene().buildIndex);
        }

        public void LoadMenu()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(menuSceneName);
        }

        private void SetPaused(bool value)
        {
            paused = value;
            Time.timeScale = paused ? 0f : 1f;
            if (pauseMenu != null) pauseMenu.SetActive(paused);
        }

        private void OnDestroy() => Time.timeScale = 1f;
    }

## Design rules
- Centralize pause ownership so multiple menus do not overwrite timeScale.
- Scaled physics/coroutines pause at timeScale zero; Update and unscaled systems can continue.
- Restore timeScale before loading another scene.
- Use Input Actions for gamepad/mobile compatibility.
- Ensure the pause UI still receives input and is not disabled with gameplay.

Common issues: scene omitted from Build Profile, incorrect menu name, pause state left at zero between scenes, or a coroutine waits on scaled time while paused.

# 3D Trigger Zones and Area Events

Trigger volumes detect objects entering, staying inside, and leaving a region. They are useful for checkpoints, doors, tutorial prompts, damage zones, and level boundaries.

## Zone component
Create a BoxCollider or SphereCollider and enable Is Trigger. Ensure the collider/Rigidbody combination satisfies Unity's trigger requirements. Assign the tag that should activate the zone.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class TriggerZone3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private string requiredTag = "Player";
        [SerializeField] private GameObject prompt;
        private bool occupied;

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (prompt != null) prompt.SetActive(false);
        }

        private void OnTriggerEnter(Collider other)
        {
            if (occupied || !other.CompareTag(requiredTag)) return;
            occupied = true;
            if (prompt != null) prompt.SetActive(true);
        }

        private void OnTriggerExit(Collider other)
        {
            if (!other.CompareTag(requiredTag)) return;
            occupied = false;
            if (prompt != null) prompt.SetActive(false);
        }
    }

This simple boolean assumes only one matching actor enters at a time. For multiplayer or multiple same-tag actors, track each entrant (for example, with a HashSet) instead of a single occupied flag. Ensure child colliders don't cause enter/exit flicker as a character moves.

## Physics and filtering
Use layers to limit which objects can trigger the zone. A trigger callback is distinct from a raycast/overlap query. For a long-lived volume, add logic for actors that are disabled/destroyed while inside so state can be cleared safely.

## Common mistakes
- Is Trigger is off.
- Rigidbody setup does not qualify for trigger callbacks.
- Required tag isn't assigned.
- Several player colliders produce repeated enters/exits.
- Prompt stays active when the actor is destroyed or disabled in the zone.

# 3D Game State: Win, Lose, and Restart Flow

A single state owner makes game flow predictable. An enum can represent Playing, Paused, Won, and Lost; separate booleans can otherwise create conflicting combinations.

## State manager
Save as GameState3D.cs. Assign win/lose panels and the main menu scene in Inspector; include the scene in the active Build Profile.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;

    public sealed class GameState3D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public enum State { Playing, Paused, Won, Lost }
        public State CurrentState { get; private set; } = State.Playing;
        [SerializeField] private GameObject winPanel;
        [SerializeField] private GameObject losePanel;
        [SerializeField] private string menuScene = "MainMenu";

        private void Start() => RefreshUI();

        public void Win()
        {
            if (CurrentState != State.Playing) return;
            CurrentState = State.Won;
            RefreshUI();
        }

        public void Lose()
        {
            if (CurrentState != State.Playing) return;
            CurrentState = State.Lost;
            RefreshUI();
        }

        public void RestartCurrentScene()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(SceneManager.GetActiveScene().buildIndex);
        }

        public void ReturnToMenu()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(menuScene);
        }

        private void RefreshUI()
        {
            if (winPanel != null) winPanel.SetActive(CurrentState == State.Won);
            if (losePanel != null) losePanel.SetActive(CurrentState == State.Lost);
        }
    }

This manages state and panel visibility only. Gate player input/AI and decide whether time should pause on win/loss in one central place. Avoid letting multiple components independently set Time.timeScale.

## Testing checklist
Call Win/Lose more than once, restart after a pause, test missing UI references, and verify scene inclusion. Decide explicitly whether pause may coexist with a terminal state.

# 3D Mouse Cursor Lock and Sensitivity

Cursor lock is useful for first-person or third-person camera control. Menus should generally unlock the cursor so buttons can be clicked. Decide how window focus and pause state should restore it.

## Cursor controller
Save as CursorLockController.cs. Connect LockCursor to the gameplay-start button and UnlockCursor to pause/menu buttons.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;

    public sealed class CursorLockController : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private bool lockOnStart = true;

        private void Start()
        {
            SetLocked(lockOnStart);
        }

        public void LockCursor() => SetLocked(true);
        public void UnlockCursor() => SetLocked(false);

        private static void SetLocked(bool locked)
        {
            Cursor.lockState = locked ? CursorLockMode.Locked : CursorLockMode.None;
            Cursor.visible = !locked;
        }

        private void OnApplicationFocus(bool hasFocus)
        {
            if (!hasFocus) SetLocked(false);
        }

        private void OnDisable() => SetLocked(false);
    }

If you keep gameplay input in a separate script, don't let both scripts independently change Cursor.lockState. Re-locking should be an intentional action after returning to gameplay; don't automatically trap the cursor as soon as focus returns if a menu is open.

## Mouse sensitivity
Mouse delta is already a per-frame displacement in the Input System. Apply sensitivity to that delta, and do not blindly multiply mouse delta by Time.deltaTime. A gamepad stick is a rate input and usually needs Time.deltaTime. Use different sensitivities for mouse and stick.

## Common mistakes
- Cursor remains locked over a settings menu.
- Cursor locks again on application focus when the player is still in a menu.
- Sensitivity is scaled twice.
- Camera input continues while paused or UI is active.
